WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.339
<c.p>[Begin Tape 4531.

2
00:00:01.339 --> 00:00:02.679
Begin Session I.]

3
00:00:02.679 --> 00:00:03.125
<v CHELSEA ARSENEAULT>Alrighty.

4
00:00:03.125 --> 00:00:05.357
Today is September 30, 2015.

5
00:00:05.357 --> 00:00:06.696
It’s a Wednesday.

6
00:00:06.696 --> 00:00:15.179
And we’re here today, I’m Chelsea Arseneault with the T. Harry Williams Center for Oral History with LSU libraries.

7
00:00:15.179 --> 00:00:22.768
I’m here today with Mr. Arthur Kenneth Lee at his beautiful home in, is this Westlake, technically?

8
00:00:22.768 --> 00:00:25.000
<v ARTHUR KENNETH LEE>No. This is Gillis.

9
00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:25.361
<v ARSENEAULT>Gillis?

10
00:00:25.361 --> 00:00:25.722
<v LEE>Yeah.

11
00:00:25.722 --> 00:00:26.083
<v ARSENEAULT>G-i-

12
00:00:26.083 --> 00:00:26.444
<v LEE>G-i-l-l-i-s.

13
00:00:26.444 --> 00:00:26.806
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

14
00:00:26.806 --> 00:00:27.167
Okay.

15
00:00:27.167 --> 00:00:27.528
<v LEE>Yeah.

16
00:00:27.528 --> 00:00:30.778
And I have some new furniture in my mouth.

17
00:00:30.778 --> 00:00:35.472
So my words might not be as clear as they normally would be.

18
00:00:35.472 --> 00:00:37.639
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, you sound good to me.

19
00:00:37.639 --> 00:00:39.806
And the audio can pick up.

20
00:00:39.806 --> 00:00:42.333
You don’t have to talk too much.

21
00:00:42.333 --> 00:00:43.417
It’s pretty sensitive.

22
00:00:43.417 --> 00:00:43.778
<v LEE>Okay.

23
00:00:43.778 --> 00:00:54.250
<v ARSENEAULT>And we’re here today to talk about your memories of Mossville as part of a project in conjunction with the Imperial Calcasieu Museum to document the history of Mossville.

24
00:00:54.250 --> 00:00:58.583
This is our first interview together, but this is your second interview.

25
00:00:58.583 --> 00:01:00.028
I think you did . . .

26
00:01:00.028 --> 00:01:00.389
<v LEE>Yes.

27
00:01:00.389 --> 00:01:03.278
<v ARSENEAULT>. . .your first one at the Oral History Day.

28
00:01:03.278 --> 00:01:04.000
<v LEE>Yes.

29
00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:05.263
<v ARSENEAULT>At the Rigmaiden Center.

30
00:01:05.263 --> 00:01:05.579
<v LEE>Correct.

31
00:01:05.579 --> 00:01:06.211
<v ARSENEAULT>All right.

32
00:01:06.211 --> 00:01:10.947
So, in your initial interview, you talked a bit about your family and their origins.

33
00:01:10.947 --> 00:01:14.737
And we’re starting to learn how everyone in the community is related.

34
00:01:14.737 --> 00:01:18.211
And we wanted to talk a little bit more about that.

35
00:01:18.211 --> 00:01:22.000
So could you tell us how you’re related to Perry Trahan?

36
00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:22.971
<v LEE>We’re brothers.

37
00:01:22.971 --> 00:01:25.398
We have the same father.

38
00:01:25.398 --> 00:01:27.825
And we have different mothers.

39
00:01:27.825 --> 00:01:30.738
My dad and mother was together.

40
00:01:30.738 --> 00:01:37.049
And then Richard and I was the only two kids from my mother.

41
00:01:37.049 --> 00:01:41.903
And my father and mother divorced at very early age.

42
00:01:41.903 --> 00:01:47.728
But my father still had a hand in raising us, you know.

43
00:01:47.728 --> 00:01:54.524
He was there and paid his child, it wasn’t really child support back then.

44
00:01:54.524 --> 00:01:58.408
But he helped us, took care of us.

45
00:01:58.408 --> 00:02:11.029
And the main thing was the things he tried to instill into us is to get a good education and the work ethics that he demanded.

46
00:02:11.029 --> 00:02:15.398
And the grades in school he demanded, you know.

47
00:02:15.398 --> 00:02:19.767
It wasn’t no C action or no D action.

48
00:02:19.767 --> 00:02:25.592
It was A and B action, or you will see me later.

49
00:02:25.592 --> 00:02:26.563
You know.

50
00:02:26.563 --> 00:02:28.505
And he stressed education.

51
00:02:28.505 --> 00:02:39.184
And he worked, and somebody else in the neighborhood was doing some hard work, my uncle ran a farm for Dr. Fellows.

52
00:02:39.184 --> 00:02:44.039
And that was, on the Jordan side of the family.

53
00:02:44.039 --> 00:02:46.951
My mama’s sister married a Jordan.

54
00:02:46.951 --> 00:02:52.291
And he worked for . . . he ran the farm for Dr. Fellows.

55
00:02:52.291 --> 00:03:02.971
And they . . . my dad and my uncle and them would see that we’d go to work with my uncle and work hard.

56
00:03:02.971 --> 00:03:08.311
And they said back then they was educating us about ignorance.

57
00:03:08.311 --> 00:03:16.563
And at the time you know, we used to kind of, hey, what’s wrong with that statement?

58
00:03:16.563 --> 00:03:20.932
Something is not . . . what the hell he talking about?

59
00:03:20.932 --> 00:03:22.874
Educate you about ignorance?

60
00:03:22.874 --> 00:03:33.068
And later on, you know, as I got older, I realized what they were saying about working hard and you know,

61
00:03:33.068 --> 00:03:40.835
you have to work hard to make a good living, and get you a good education.

62
00:03:40.835 --> 00:03:52.971
It’s very possible if the opportunity come up you’ll be qualified in order to, you know, do some other kind of work besides manual labor.

63
00:03:52.971 --> 00:03:58.796
<c.p>Because that was one of the things throughout my life is work.

64
00:03:58.796 --> 00:04:02.680
I worked two jobs most of my life.

65
00:04:02.680 --> 00:04:07.534
Had a business on the side and stuff like that.

66
00:04:07.534 --> 00:04:12.874
Ran a club, a little club called The Pub in Mossville.

67
00:04:12.874 --> 00:04:16.272
And me and one of my friends.

68
00:04:16.272 --> 00:04:19.184
And I worked in law enforcement.

69
00:04:19.184 --> 00:04:22.097
I worked two jobs twenty-one years.

70
00:04:22.097 --> 00:04:25.495
The same two jobs for twenty-one years.

71
00:04:25.495 --> 00:04:30.350
And prior to that, I worked for the post office.

72
00:04:30.350 --> 00:04:32.291
Worked for the government.

73
00:04:32.291 --> 00:04:34.233
For the federal government.

74
00:04:34.233 --> 00:04:38.602
And I did hustling on the side, you know.

75
00:04:38.602 --> 00:04:42.000
And I opened a little club.

76
00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:49.148
And I worked, you know, here and there, at odds and ends coming up.

77
00:04:49.148 --> 00:04:52.722
Jimmy Comeaux and I were good friends.

78
00:04:52.722 --> 00:04:58.339
And we worked for [Gene and Dylon Kile], the trucking company.

79
00:04:58.339 --> 00:05:02.424
And [Comeaux] and them had a filling station.

80
00:05:02.424 --> 00:05:04.976
And we was young kids.

81
00:05:04.976 --> 00:05:11.104
And we’d go work, you know, at the station on the weekends.

82
00:05:11.104 --> 00:05:17.741
In the afternoons we wasn’t playing, or practicing for football, basketball, for sports,

83
00:05:17.741 --> 00:05:24.889
then we would go work and make money and purchase things that we wanted.

84
00:05:24.889 --> 00:05:31.527
Because I feel like I was lucky because I was in eighth grade.

85
00:05:31.527 --> 00:05:36.633
And I had enough money, I bought me a car.

86
00:05:36.633 --> 00:05:40.718
You know, and my dad signed for me.

87
00:05:40.718 --> 00:05:42.249
He bought it.

88
00:05:42.249 --> 00:05:55.014
I mean, I paid for it, but I had to have the money for the insurance and the car, but I had no driver’s license.

89
00:05:55.014 --> 00:05:59.099
So you know, it wasn’t that much traffic.

90
00:05:59.099 --> 00:06:05.226
And I was limited to where I could go in the car.

91
00:06:05.226 --> 00:06:08.800
Anyway, to work and school and stuff.

92
00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:13.906
At night, all through high school, I had a vehicle.

93
00:06:13.906 --> 00:06:21.565
And felt like I was lucky in the, you know, the work and things that,

94
00:06:21.565 --> 00:06:29.734
work and be able to contribute to the material things that you could get and accomplish.

95
00:06:29.734 --> 00:06:35.351
And I tried to instill the same thing into my children.

96
00:06:35.351 --> 00:06:39.946
My children, their values and work ethics and education.

97
00:06:39.946 --> 00:06:51.689
They all went to college and, you know, one has a doctor’s and the other one is working on her nurse practitioner license.

98
00:06:51.689 --> 00:06:57.816
And my son, he works for the parish as a tax assessor.

99
00:06:57.816 --> 00:07:05.986
And you know, it gives you a good feeling, you know, that you did accomplish something.

100
00:07:05.986 --> 00:07:11.092
And you know, Mossville, in that we use community raise.

101
00:07:11.092 --> 00:07:13.134
And you know, people,

102
00:07:13.134 --> 00:07:18.751
<c.p>you’d come home . . .I’m kind of rattling around, back and forth.

103
00:07:18.751 --> 00:07:30.494
But you know, you’d go to school and you’d go in the high school there, you’d play ball and people would see you.

104
00:07:30.494 --> 00:07:34.068
And you know, “Y’all going to Shreveport?

105
00:07:34.068 --> 00:07:36.111
Here’s a couple dollars.

106
00:07:36.111 --> 00:07:38.153
Where you all going?

107
00:07:38.153 --> 00:07:38.664
Here.”

108
00:07:38.664 --> 00:07:45.301
And they, the pride in the community, in the school, that, you know,

109
00:07:45.301 --> 00:07:53.981
during my period there I was able to see Mossville High School win the state football championship,

110
00:07:53.981 --> 00:08:03.682
basketball championship, track and tennis and, you know, my brother’s record with the shotput stood for years and years.

111
00:08:03.682 --> 00:08:06.746
And when the school was segregated.

112
00:08:06.746 --> 00:08:16.447
And I don't think his record was ever broken during, and after they integrated, it might have got broken.

113
00:08:16.447 --> 00:08:19.000
But at that time . . .

114
00:08:19.000 --> 00:08:20.748
<v ARSENEAULT>Is that Mr. Richard?

115
00:08:20.748 --> 00:08:21.185
<v LEE>Yes.

116
00:08:21.185 --> 00:08:23.369
<v ARSENEAULT>He had the shotput record?

117
00:08:23.369 --> 00:08:23.806
<v LEE>Yeah.

118
00:08:23.806 --> 00:08:25.991
He had the shotput record.

119
00:08:25.991 --> 00:08:35.603
And I, you know, I played, I was a [trainer, and book-keeper for the football team] during the days he was there.

120
00:08:35.603 --> 00:08:38.225
And he was ahead of me.

121
00:08:38.225 --> 00:08:47.837
And I won the police Olympics with the shotput, you know, in Baton Rouge, the state, I worked for the sheriff’s department.

122
00:08:47.837 --> 00:08:51.332
And they had a Olympics, the police Olympics.

123
00:08:51.332 --> 00:08:53.517
And I won the shotput.

124
00:08:53.517 --> 00:09:00.071
And you know, the year before that, they’d say, “Well, they threw thirty-five, forty feet.”

125
00:09:00.071 --> 00:09:01.382
I said, “What?!

126
00:09:01.382 --> 00:09:03.129
I can do that.”

127
00:09:03.129 --> 00:09:04.003
You know?

128
00:09:04.003 --> 00:09:07.935
And I said, “Man, I could just front that.”

129
00:09:07.935 --> 00:09:12.305
And they said, “Oh, man, you just can’t do that.”

130
00:09:12.305 --> 00:09:13.615
<c.p>So I tried.

131
00:09:13.615 --> 00:09:14.926
I showed them.

132
00:09:14.926 --> 00:09:19.295
I said, “Mike [Williams], come on, man, Mike, will you?”

133
00:09:19.295 --> 00:09:21.917
And we went to Sulphur High.

134
00:09:21.917 --> 00:09:25.412
And I shot it out there, 48 feet.

135
00:09:25.412 --> 00:09:26.723
Mike said, “Man!”

136
00:09:26.723 --> 00:09:29.345
He say, “We going to win!”

137
00:09:29.345 --> 00:09:31.966
I say, “What’s that ‘we’ thing?”

138
00:09:31.966 --> 00:09:32.840
You know?

139
00:09:32.840 --> 00:09:36.772
But I learned that hanging around there with Richard.

140
00:09:36.772 --> 00:09:43.326
We had, John Reado was in my class, a grade ahead of me, behind me.

141
00:09:43.326 --> 00:09:47.695
And he was, him and my brother would throw together.

142
00:09:47.695 --> 00:09:52.938
But Richard would practice at home and I’d mess around with him.

143
00:09:52.938 --> 00:09:55.123
And Mossville was just . . . man.

144
00:09:55.123 --> 00:10:02.114
It’s not enough time in the day for me to say and state the things that,

145
00:10:02.114 --> 00:10:06.920
you know, as a kid, a young kid, you wanted horses.

146
00:10:06.920 --> 00:10:08.231
I liked horses.

147
00:10:08.231 --> 00:10:09.105
My passion.

148
00:10:09.105 --> 00:10:13.037
Well, my uncle and I got some out there.

149
00:10:13.037 --> 00:10:21.338
My uncle and several of my cousins, he told us to go watch the horses, see how they move.

150
00:10:21.338 --> 00:10:23.523
It was during the summer.

151
00:10:23.523 --> 00:10:24.834
School was out.

152
00:10:24.834 --> 00:10:29.640
And he said, “We’re going to, you all go study them.”

153
00:10:29.640 --> 00:10:35.320
And he said, “We’re going to go and catch some for you all.”

154
00:10:35.320 --> 00:10:36.631
And we did.

155
00:10:36.631 --> 00:10:41.000
We went and watched them for about two weeks.

156
00:10:41.000 --> 00:10:49.800
And then one Saturday morning, they got up, my uncle and one of my first cousins, [Dickie] Prater.

157
00:10:49.800 --> 00:10:56.644
And we went out there in the woods up off of Evergreen up there.

158
00:10:56.644 --> 00:11:02.022
And sure enough, we went and caught, captured some wild horses.

159
00:11:02.022 --> 00:11:11.311
And everybody got one that was, and things like that, man, it was a learning experience and, you know,

160
00:11:11.311 --> 00:11:17.667
it contributed a lot of my survival in the service was you know,

161
00:11:17.667 --> 00:11:24.511
we’d go camp out there and watch the horses all day and track them.

162
00:11:24.511 --> 00:11:27.933
You could tell where they had passed.

163
00:11:27.933 --> 00:11:32.333
And if a bell rang, you’d go out there.

164
00:11:32.333 --> 00:11:43.578
In the service, and in training and everything, and you could see, it all came back into well, I learned this at home.

165
00:11:43.578 --> 00:11:50.911
That you can tell through the dew or the bushes that something passed through here.

166
00:11:50.911 --> 00:11:51.889
You know?

167
00:11:51.889 --> 00:11:54.333
And it rings your bell.

168
00:11:54.333 --> 00:11:59.222
And I don't know, Mossville was, man, it’s, you know,

169
00:11:59.222 --> 00:12:10.467
things happen throughout your life and you can relate back to the community activities and the people and, you know, and I was

170
00:12:10.467 --> 00:12:14.378
<c.p>the law over there for 15, 16 years.

171
00:12:14.378 --> 00:12:18.289
I was the resident deputy there in Mossville.

172
00:12:18.289 --> 00:12:26.600
And very seldom I took a kind of a different approach to where I would, you know,

173
00:12:26.600 --> 00:12:34.422
if I hear of, or see something that’s going on, I would go to your father.

174
00:12:34.422 --> 00:12:40.289
And talk to him, to try to get him to correct it.

175
00:12:40.289 --> 00:12:43.711
If it wasn’t something major, you know?

176
00:12:43.711 --> 00:12:50.067
And I felt like that way, you know, everybody deserve a second chance.

177
00:12:50.067 --> 00:12:59.844
And if it wasn’t really that he hurted somebody or something like that, I kind of used a different approach.

178
00:12:59.844 --> 00:13:04.733
And sometimes they maybe took Uncle Harry’s lawnmower or something.

179
00:13:04.733 --> 00:13:08.644
I’m just using these names [as an example].

180
00:13:08.644 --> 00:13:13.044
And I’d go to the father and say, “Look.

181
00:13:13.044 --> 00:13:15.978
I don’t have the bare facts.

182
00:13:15.978 --> 00:13:22.822
But I heard your son, your grandson, was involved in stealing Uncle Harry’s lawnmower.

183
00:13:22.822 --> 00:13:23.800
You understand?

184
00:13:23.800 --> 00:13:31.622
I’m sure they didn’t steal it, they just borrowed it to go cut some grass somewhere.

185
00:13:31.622 --> 00:13:34.067
Make them a little change.

186
00:13:34.067 --> 00:13:38.467
And they forgot to bring it back, you know?

187
00:13:38.467 --> 00:13:44.822
And I want you to go see about getting Uncle Harry’s lawnmower back.”

188
00:13:44.822 --> 00:13:53.133
And nine out of ten, Uncle Harry would get his lawnmower back, you know what I’m saying?

189
00:13:53.133 --> 00:13:59.000
Now he’s got to go cut up Uncle Harry’s yard for . . .

190
00:13:59.000 --> 00:13:59.968
<v ARSENEAULT>A month?

191
00:13:59.968 --> 00:14:00.452
<v LEE>Yeah.

192
00:14:00.452 --> 00:14:05.774
Not necessarily a month but you know, some kind of punishment.

193
00:14:05.774 --> 00:14:11.097
And you know, it, you know, it was a community effort.

194
00:14:11.097 --> 00:14:19.323
And you see, because I don’t want to pull up at your house with the police car,

195
00:14:19.323 --> 00:14:25.613
detective, to go there to arrest your son about something, or your grandson.

196
00:14:25.613 --> 00:14:28.032
And it’s a bad image.

197
00:14:28.032 --> 00:14:30.935
But somebody got to do it.

198
00:14:30.935 --> 00:14:36.742
And this is the way I’m going to try to do it.

199
00:14:36.742 --> 00:14:39.161
Now I’ll go do it.

200
00:14:39.161 --> 00:14:41.581
Now don’t get me wrong.

201
00:14:41.581 --> 00:14:48.839
But if you can correct this, you know, I think John deserves a better chance.

202
00:14:48.839 --> 00:14:55.613
To let him know that he can’t get away with this kind of activities.

203
00:14:55.613 --> 00:14:59.484
But, and that’s what Mossville, that was family.

204
00:14:59.484 --> 00:15:00.452
You know?

205
00:15:00.452 --> 00:15:07.710
<c.p>And I have 100 uncles out there and then maybe half, about three or four.

206
00:15:07.710 --> 00:15:15.452
But everybody was an uncle, Uncle John or Uncle Bob or something like that, you know?

207
00:15:15.452 --> 00:15:18.355
But they wasn’t really blood uncles.

208
00:15:18.355 --> 00:15:25.129
Just, you know, when, and you know, you get in trouble, you know, kids,

209
00:15:25.129 --> 00:15:31.903
kid thing, you got to be fighting out there, playing ball and start fighting.

210
00:15:31.903 --> 00:15:35.290
Well, Aunt Lorraine going to whoop you.

211
00:15:35.290 --> 00:15:41.581
You know, get down there and [John Prater’s] mama seen some of it.

212
00:15:41.581 --> 00:15:44.000
John was involved in it.

213
00:15:44.000 --> 00:15:45.935
And I’m with John.

214
00:15:45.935 --> 00:15:49.323
She’d pass you a couple of licks.

215
00:15:49.323 --> 00:15:55.129
And the telephone would beat you to Big Mama’s house, you know?

216
00:15:55.129 --> 00:15:57.548
The whole village raise you.

217
00:15:57.548 --> 00:16:01.419
And it don’t happen like that no more.

218
00:16:01.419 --> 00:16:03.839
It don’t happen like that.

219
00:16:03.839 --> 00:16:10.613
And it was a harsh discipline, I think, but you know, I’m a disciplinarian.

220
00:16:10.613 --> 00:16:13.032
I like, you know, things.

221
00:16:13.032 --> 00:16:17.871
But abuse and everything got kind of out of hand.

222
00:16:17.871 --> 00:16:24.161
And the kids using it on their parents is in reverse, you know.

223
00:16:24.161 --> 00:16:32.387
They do something and you try to chastise them for it, you end up getting in trouble.

224
00:16:32.387 --> 00:16:36.258
But Mossville, it was a whole different ballgame.

225
00:16:36.258 --> 00:16:39.161
It was a whole different ballgame.

226
00:16:39.161 --> 00:16:47.541
Because my daddy would just still be in jail because he would put it on.

227
00:16:47.541 --> 00:16:50.492
You know what I’m saying?

228
00:16:50.492 --> 00:16:57.574
But life in Mossville, it can’t, I keep saying that, but man,

229
00:16:57.574 --> 00:17:06.426
there’s so many things that happened in the school and in the system at Mossville.

230
00:17:06.426 --> 00:17:09.377
And I graduated in 1966.

231
00:17:09.377 --> 00:17:13.508
I was the president of my class.

232
00:17:13.508 --> 00:17:17.049
And captain of the basketball team.

233
00:17:17.049 --> 00:17:20.000
And went to Grambling.

234
00:17:20.000 --> 00:17:23.810
<v ARSENEAULT>What year did Mossville High School get built?

235
00:17:23.810 --> 00:17:26.667
<v LEE>I think it was around ’57.

236
00:17:26.667 --> 00:17:28.095
I’m not sure.

237
00:17:28.095 --> 00:17:28.571
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

238
00:17:28.571 --> 00:17:31.429
<v LEE>It was in the late ‘50s.

239
00:17:31.429 --> 00:17:39.048
And my aunty, Kim to Mama, Aunt Julia was in one of the first graduate classes.

240
00:17:39.048 --> 00:17:42.857
<v ARSENEAULT>So that’s your, how are you related to . . .

241
00:17:42.857 --> 00:17:46.667
<v LEE>Kim’s mother, Aunt Julia, is my mother’s sister.

242
00:17:46.667 --> 00:17:49.048
<v ARSENEAULT>What’s your mom’s last name?

243
00:17:49.048 --> 00:17:52.381
<v LEE>My mom’s name was Elaine Lemelle Lee.

244
00:17:52.381 --> 00:17:52.857
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

245
00:17:52.857 --> 00:17:54.762
So she’s a Lemelle.

246
00:17:54.762 --> 00:17:56.667
<v LEE>She was a Lemelle.

247
00:17:56.667 --> 00:17:57.143
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

248
00:17:57.143 --> 00:18:00.000
<v LEE>Kim’s mama was a Lemelle.

249
00:18:00.000 --> 00:18:00.551
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

250
00:18:00.551 --> 00:18:01.103
Right.

251
00:18:01.103 --> 00:18:03.308
There’s five girls, right?

252
00:18:03.308 --> 00:18:07.720
And then Uncle Butch is the only boy.

253
00:18:07.720 --> 00:18:08.271
<v LEE>Yeah.

254
00:18:08.271 --> 00:18:11.028
That’s the way it was.

255
00:18:11.028 --> 00:18:14.336
<v ARSENEAULT>And your dad was a Lee.

256
00:18:14.336 --> 00:18:14.888
<v LEE>Yeah.

257
00:18:14.888 --> 00:18:16.542
<v ARSENEAULT>And his parents?

258
00:18:16.542 --> 00:18:23.159
<v LEE>Were Jim Lee and Alda was an [Olivier?] before she married Jim.

259
00:18:23.159 --> 00:18:23.710
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

260
00:18:23.710 --> 00:18:33.636
<v LEE>My grandfather Jim, he was a stevedore [worked on the waterfront loading and unloading ships at the docks].

261
00:18:33.636 --> 00:18:45.766
And he was like the, like what they call now the business agent, he was like a business agent for the blacks.

262
00:18:45.766 --> 00:18:50.729
And the docks [port] was over where PPG was.

263
00:18:50.729 --> 00:18:54.589
What they called back then Bayou D’Inde.

264
00:18:54.589 --> 00:18:57.346
<v ARSENEAULT>Stevedore was his last name?

265
00:18:57.346 --> 00:18:59.000
<v LEE>Jim Lee.

266
00:18:59.000 --> 00:18:59.844
<v ARSENEAULT>Jim Lee?

267
00:18:59.844 --> 00:19:01.111
<v LEE>Was my grandfather.

268
00:19:01.111 --> 00:19:01.533
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

269
00:19:01.533 --> 00:19:07.022
<v LEE>And he was like the stevedores like they unload the ships and stuff.

270
00:19:07.022 --> 00:19:07.867
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay, okay.

271
00:19:07.867 --> 00:19:09.133
I got you.

272
00:19:09.133 --> 00:19:10.822
<v LEE>The docks and everything.

273
00:19:10.822 --> 00:19:11.244
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

274
00:19:11.244 --> 00:19:12.089
I see.

275
00:19:12.089 --> 00:19:15.889
<v LEE>And that’s what he, he retired off the waterfront.

276
00:19:15.889 --> 00:19:20.533
And went to the docks were back there by Bayou D’Inde.

277
00:19:20.533 --> 00:19:25.178
And we would Bayou D’Inde to Mossville, Mossville to Bayou D’Inde.

278
00:19:25.178 --> 00:19:29.822
And Jim was the business agent for the blacks back then.

279
00:19:29.822 --> 00:19:31.511
<v ARSENEAULT>What does that mean?

280
00:19:31.511 --> 00:19:37.000
<v LEE>Like he was the head man, like a foreman or a supervisor.

281
00:19:37.000 --> 00:19:41.479
<v ARSENEAULT>So there were black workers and white, they were separated?

282
00:19:41.479 --> 00:19:44.166
<v LEE>Yeah, black workers and white workers.

283
00:19:44.166 --> 00:19:52.228
And he also, him and Marvin White purchased some land from Grandpa that right there on Highway 90,

284
00:19:52.228 --> 00:19:56.259
right there by Mossville, they had the Midway Drive-in.

285
00:19:56.259 --> 00:20:02.977
And my mom and my grandfather and them sold the land and my mama ran

286
00:20:02.977 --> 00:20:07.456
<c.p>the kitchen, they had the black and the white side.

287
00:20:07.456 --> 00:20:16.414
My mama and a couple of my cousins cooked and sold food on the black side for the drive-in movie.

288
00:20:16.414 --> 00:20:22.237
And a lot of the people, that was where we’d go for Mossville.

289
00:20:22.237 --> 00:20:24.924
We’d go to the picture show.

290
00:20:24.924 --> 00:20:32.986
And it was right, it was just east of Prater Road on the south side of the road.

291
00:20:32.986 --> 00:20:41.048
And see my grandfather had a 200-acre strip from there all the way back to where PPG was.

292
00:20:41.048 --> 00:20:50.454
The Jupiter Chemical, where they had the killing in the ‘70s at Jupiter, that was in our own backyard back then.

293
00:20:50.454 --> 00:20:51.797
<v ARSENEAULT>Who got killed?

294
00:20:51.797 --> 00:20:53.589
<v LEE>What his name was.

295
00:20:53.589 --> 00:21:02.099
I want to say, one of the, I don’t want to call his name, I might call it wrong.

296
00:21:02.099 --> 00:21:04.786
But they was having union/non-union troubles.

297
00:21:04.786 --> 00:21:06.130
Union and non-union.

298
00:21:06.130 --> 00:21:09.265
And one of the people got killed.

299
00:21:09.265 --> 00:21:11.504
They were shooting and all.

300
00:21:11.504 --> 00:21:16.879
It wasn’t really trying to kill him, but they were just shooting.

301
00:21:16.879 --> 00:21:19.566
And he got hit and killed.

302
00:21:19.566 --> 00:21:24.045
And but that was in our own backyard back there.

303
00:21:24.045 --> 00:21:30.315
And because, you know, a bunch of us would get together from Mossville there.

304
00:21:30.315 --> 00:21:36.138
And we’d cut through the woods and go to the movies, you know?

305
00:21:36.138 --> 00:21:39.273
We’re up always running it, you know.

306
00:21:39.273 --> 00:21:47.783
And you know, the nights that I didn’t have to go at work, I made popcorn, and boxed popcorn.

307
00:21:47.783 --> 00:21:52.262
And then some of my buddies would come meet me.

308
00:21:52.262 --> 00:21:54.949
My grandfather would let them in.

309
00:21:54.949 --> 00:21:58.980
And then we’d stand around and watch the movie.

310
00:21:58.980 --> 00:22:03.907
Then I had a car, and they would come meet me.

311
00:22:03.907 --> 00:22:16.000
And we’d sit in the car after, I had to keep checking the popcorn machine and make sure they have some boxes of popcorn on sale.

312
00:22:16.000 --> 00:22:25.749
And it’s so much that went on that from time to time comes through my mind about, there don’t be no more.

313
00:22:25.749 --> 00:22:26.635
You know?

314
00:22:26.635 --> 00:22:29.737
But the memories is there, you know.

315
00:22:29.737 --> 00:22:39.042
To drive down the road and say Uncle Harry used to live there, you know, I remember when Ms. Aida whooped

316
00:22:39.042 --> 00:22:40.371
<c.p>me over here.

317
00:22:40.371 --> 00:22:42.587
I had done something wrong.

318
00:22:42.587 --> 00:22:46.132
I threw a stick in my brother’s spokes.

319
00:22:46.132 --> 00:22:47.461
I wasn’t trying.

320
00:22:47.461 --> 00:22:54.994
I was throwing it at him, but I hit the bike and it went in the spokes.

321
00:22:54.994 --> 00:22:58.539
And my aunty, was rude to aunty [Ms.

322
00:22:58.539 --> 00:23:02.970
Aida], and Aunt Eunice was talking, and they seen that.

323
00:23:02.970 --> 00:23:05.629
No doubt, you know what happened.

324
00:23:05.629 --> 00:23:08.731
And my brother fell, scratched his arm.

325
00:23:08.731 --> 00:23:15.377
He thought he was going to die the way he was crying and going on.

326
00:23:15.377 --> 00:23:21.581
And then he looked and Eunice was whooping me, I could see him smiling.

327
00:23:21.581 --> 00:23:30.000
And I said, “You bastard,” I said, you know. (laughs) [Lee clarifies he said this under his breath].

328
00:23:30.000 --> 00:23:32.459
<v ARSENEAULT>That made him feel better.

329
00:23:32.459 --> 00:23:34.918
<v LEE>It made him feel better.

330
00:23:34.918 --> 00:23:39.836
But I got a couple whoopings that day about that.

331
00:23:39.836 --> 00:23:45.738
I don't know, man, it’s a joyful, happy feeling, because, you know,

332
00:23:45.738 --> 00:23:56.557
you’ve got a chance to upgrade your standards of living, if I had the money and the purchasing or selling and everything.

333
00:23:56.557 --> 00:24:02.459
And good for me might not be good for the next person.

334
00:24:02.459 --> 00:24:07.377
But it was good for me, and good to me.

335
00:24:07.377 --> 00:24:13.770
Because I moved up here [to Moss Bluff] 16 years ago, you understand?

336
00:24:13.770 --> 00:24:18.197
I was in one of the buyouts with Conoco.

337
00:24:18.197 --> 00:24:25.082
And I purchased this house and all this other work, the house wasn’t finished.

338
00:24:25.082 --> 00:24:30.492
All the rest of this I did, through contracting it out.

339
00:24:30.492 --> 00:24:36.393
Some of it I did, and some of it my friends did.

340
00:24:36.393 --> 00:24:46.230
Like this, I took the fence up I had in Mossville, that [three rail] white fence and all of that.

341
00:24:46.230 --> 00:24:52.131
It’s given you a chance to really, you know, you can upgrade.

342
00:24:52.131 --> 00:25:02.459
And to me, that’s one of the challenges of life is to, you know, don’t be stagnant and close-minded about things.

343
00:25:02.459 --> 00:25:04.426
Yeah, we’re losing Mossville.

344
00:25:04.426 --> 00:25:09.836
But yet, we’re gaining success in upgrading the standards of living.

345
00:25:09.836 --> 00:25:17.705
And some things that you probably ordinarily wouldn’t have been able to purchase through God and,

346
00:25:17.705 --> 00:25:25.574
you know, things, have given you this opportunity to better yourself and better your living conditions.

347
00:25:25.574 --> 00:25:26.066
And

348
00:25:26.066 --> 00:25:28.033
<c.p>your standards of living.

349
00:25:28.033 --> 00:25:30.984
It’s a win/win subject to me.

350
00:25:30.984 --> 00:25:39.344
If I had eight brothers and a little small piece of land, I could maybe say, well,

351
00:25:39.344 --> 00:25:51.639
and I’m living on that one little piece of land, and I’ve got to split it with eight people, I wouldn’t be happy, you know?

352
00:25:51.639 --> 00:25:58.525
Because that’s what I tell, you know, Kenny, what, I say, “Wait a minute.

353
00:25:58.525 --> 00:26:08.852
Before we start discussing this about Sasol or this and that, what’s good for you might not be good for me.

354
00:26:08.852 --> 00:26:14.262
And what’s good for me, might not be good for you.”

355
00:26:14.262 --> 00:26:18.197
I said, “That’s why they call it life.”

356
00:26:18.197 --> 00:26:19.180
You understand?

357
00:26:19.180 --> 00:26:26.066
And I say to you, all kind of challenges going to come at you.”

358
00:26:26.066 --> 00:26:30.000
I say, “Just like the devil and God.

359
00:26:30.000 --> 00:26:31.967
You got a choice.

360
00:26:31.967 --> 00:26:39.836
You want to go with the devil, or you want to try to go with God.”

361
00:26:39.836 --> 00:26:40.820
You know?

362
00:26:40.820 --> 00:26:49.672
Because he ain’t going to put you nowhere that you, you know, the grace of God won’t [unclear].

363
00:26:49.672 --> 00:26:54.098
And I said, to me, this is a blessing.

364
00:26:54.098 --> 00:27:00.000
And that’s the way I’m looking at it, as a blessing.

365
00:27:00.000 --> 00:27:06.886
Now you take it like you want, you know, and do what you can do.

366
00:27:06.886 --> 00:27:13.772
But I thank God that he let me get out of there when I did,

367
00:27:13.772 --> 00:27:20.200
and gave me a chance to get up here and do this, you know?

368
00:27:20.200 --> 00:27:25.709
And now, what you going to do with yours, that’s your business.

369
00:27:25.709 --> 00:27:26.627
You understand?

370
00:27:26.627 --> 00:27:30.758
And if you want to stay there, stay there.

371
00:27:30.758 --> 00:27:42.695
But I say you all been griping about this water, you know, in Mossville, and this, that and the other every time a plant blew up.

372
00:27:42.695 --> 00:27:48.663
You fall out the bed or your plumbing won’t stay in the wall.

373
00:27:48.663 --> 00:27:51.417
Here’s your chance to get out.

374
00:27:51.417 --> 00:27:57.844
You know, and as I say, I left it, I said, I love Mossville,

375
00:27:57.844 --> 00:28:03.812
I love the people in Mossville, but they can’t take the memories away.

376
00:28:03.812 --> 00:28:15.289
But the, what you would say, back, the structures and all of that can be tore down, but they can’t tear them down in here.

377
00:28:15.289 --> 00:28:19.880
And I say, that’s where you keep things like that.

378
00:28:19.880 --> 00:28:25.848
I say, just like your brother died, they can’t take that from you.

379
00:28:25.848 --> 00:28:34.571
They might could take your brother from you, but they can’t take the memories of your brother from you.

380
00:28:34.571 --> 00:28:39.621
And just like they used to say, get your good education.

381
00:28:39.621 --> 00:28:51.098
You might not can get the job you want, but you got the education you need to get the job if the job becomes available.

382
00:28:51.098 --> 00:28:52.016
You know?

383
00:28:52.016 --> 00:29:03.493
<c.p>And some of my friends, on report card day, (laughs) “Man, we going by Kenny’s house, you know, see what kind of grades we got.

384
00:29:03.493 --> 00:29:10.379
Because if he got some bad grades, his daddy going to whip him,” you know?

385
00:29:10.379 --> 00:29:21.397
And any of them kids that come there, that would come play at the house, they had to show their report card, you know?

386
00:29:21.397 --> 00:29:30.120
If you going to go to the store, Daddy come, sometime he’d come on the weekend and take us,

387
00:29:30.120 --> 00:29:37.465
go get ice cream or something, and the kids be there playing, he’d bring them, too.

388
00:29:37.465 --> 00:29:44.810
Well, like he tell them, he say, “When’s the last time I seen your report card?”

389
00:29:44.810 --> 00:29:51.238
He said, “Now, you know, I come check Kenny and them every six weeks.

390
00:29:51.238 --> 00:30:02.715
Just because me and his mama can’t get along, that don’t mean I’m not going to be there to see that he get his education.”

391
00:30:02.715 --> 00:30:10.978
“Well…” “He said, “Come on this time,” but he say, “next six weeks” . . . he had it down pat,

392
00:30:10.978 --> 00:30:16.946
he could tell you the date and everything . . . “I’ll be back over here.

393
00:30:16.946 --> 00:30:24.750
And if I don’t see you, or you don’t make arrangements for me to see your card,

394
00:30:24.750 --> 00:30:32.555
the next time I come to pick them up to go to the movies or go wherever,

395
00:30:32.555 --> 00:30:37.605
to the baseball game, or wherever we going, you not going.

396
00:30:37.605 --> 00:30:42.196
So it would behoove you to show me your card.

397
00:30:42.196 --> 00:30:44.950
And to have a good card.

398
00:30:44.950 --> 00:30:50.000
Because that’s going to be the key to your success.”

399
00:30:50.000 --> 00:30:59.474
And that has been a key thing with my kids that I contribute to is that Mossville raising.

400
00:30:59.474 --> 00:31:12.632
And you know, everybody pushed for you know, you to go to school and, you know, like my uncle, my great-uncles and all of them,

401
00:31:12.632 --> 00:31:23.158
my grandfather, he’s had to quit school at an early age to help send his brothers and sisters in school.

402
00:31:23.158 --> 00:31:25.263
And his dad died.

403
00:31:25.263 --> 00:31:38.421
Well, all them got their doctors, and United States ambassadors and professors at universities and everything, and it all was because of education, you know?

404
00:31:38.421 --> 00:31:51.053
Got a chance to go to a college [Grambling State University] where my uncle was an instructor there, before I got there, you know?

405
00:31:51.053 --> 00:32:01.053
But it gives you a good feeling to know that they have kind of paved the road for you.

406
00:32:01.053 --> 00:32:07.895
And you could live by example, by seeing the things that they accomplished.

407
00:32:07.895 --> 00:32:11.579
And it made you want to accomplish

408
00:32:11.579 --> 00:32:13.158
<c.p>more, you know?

409
00:32:13.158 --> 00:32:24.211
And you and your friends in Mossville, we’d be talking about this and we had, “Oh, Artis [LeDoux] is a welder.

410
00:32:24.211 --> 00:32:29.474
“Yeah, man, I think I want to be a welder!”

411
00:32:29.474 --> 00:32:35.263
Well, you know, you need to start hanging out with Artis.

412
00:32:35.263 --> 00:32:38.947
You know, he’s going to welding school.

413
00:32:38.947 --> 00:32:50.000
And just a lot of, my Uncle Frederick [Ida Mae Lemelle’s brother], my great-uncle, they had a cabinet shop there.

414
00:32:50.000 --> 00:32:51.057
<v ARSENEAULT>In Mossville?

415
00:32:51.057 --> 00:32:52.115
<v LEE>In Mossville.

416
00:32:52.115 --> 00:32:54.230
<v ARSENEAULT>What was it called?

417
00:32:54.230 --> 00:32:55.816
Do you remember?

418
00:32:55.816 --> 00:32:59.517
<v LEE>Was it the Towner Brothers Cabinet Shop?

419
00:32:59.517 --> 00:33:02.690
<v ARSENEAULT>So you’re related to the Towners?

420
00:33:02.690 --> 00:33:03.218
<v LEE>Yeah.

421
00:33:03.218 --> 00:33:11.678
Butch and their mama, Big Mama, was originally, Big Mama Lemelle, her maiden name was Towner.

422
00:33:11.678 --> 00:33:12.207
T-O-W-N-E-R.

423
00:33:12.207 --> 00:33:12.736
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

424
00:33:12.736 --> 00:33:16.966
So are you related to Ms. Lenoria Ambrose?

425
00:33:16.966 --> 00:33:17.494
<v LEE>Yes.

426
00:33:17.494 --> 00:33:18.023
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

427
00:33:18.023 --> 00:33:18.552
<v LEE>Yeah.

428
00:33:18.552 --> 00:33:21.724
<v ARSENEAULT>Because she’s a Towner, too, right?

429
00:33:21.724 --> 00:33:22.253
<v LEE>Yeah.

430
00:33:22.253 --> 00:33:24.897
<v ARSENEAULT>So are you all cousins?

431
00:33:24.897 --> 00:33:25.425
<v LEE>Cousins.

432
00:33:25.425 --> 00:33:25.954
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

433
00:33:25.954 --> 00:33:30.713
<v LEE>Her grandfather and my grandmother were brother and sister.

434
00:33:30.713 --> 00:33:34.414
See, that’s what I’m saying, the whole . . .

435
00:33:34.414 --> 00:33:36.000
<c.p>?: You okay?

436
00:33:36.000 --> 00:33:39.165
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, yeah, thank you, I’m good.

437
00:33:39.165 --> 00:33:40.220
<v LEE>Everybody there . . .

438
00:33:40.220 --> 00:33:42.330
<v ARSENEAULT>He’s talking too much.

439
00:33:42.330 --> 00:33:44.967
He’s not drinking his. (laughs)

440
00:33:44.967 --> 00:33:53.407
<v LEE>. . .was related one way or, and then there’s kind of the thirteen or fourteen original families,

441
00:33:53.407 --> 00:34:02.901
kind of intermarried, you know, married this one, you know, married a Braxton, this one married this one.

442
00:34:02.901 --> 00:34:09.758
And so it all, the circle just got bigger and bigger and bigger.

443
00:34:09.758 --> 00:34:13.451
Because Uncle Frederick did the cabinet work.

444
00:34:13.451 --> 00:34:17.143
Uncle Johnny I learned the tile work.

445
00:34:17.143 --> 00:34:20.835
And Uncle Arthur, which is Lenoria’s grandfather . . .

446
00:34:20.835 --> 00:34:24.000
<v ARSENEAULT>So are you named after him?

447
00:34:24.000 --> 00:34:24.527
<v LEE>Yeah.

448
00:34:24.527 --> 00:34:25.055
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

449
00:34:25.055 --> 00:34:31.385
<v LEE>Uncle Arthur, and he was like the structure itself, like the framing.

450
00:34:31.385 --> 00:34:35.077
And they all worked together back then.

451
00:34:35.077 --> 00:34:46.681
And at night Uncle Frederick would, we’d go by the shop, they had a cabinet shop right there, where old Bell 7/11.

452
00:34:46.681 --> 00:34:50.374
It was on the Old Spanish Trail.

453
00:34:50.374 --> 00:34:53.011
Right across from that church.

454
00:34:53.011 --> 00:34:58.813
Because Kim, that was Grandma Towner’s property that Kim had got.

455
00:34:58.813 --> 00:35:02.505
And Kim donated it to the church.

456
00:35:02.505 --> 00:35:09.363
And that’s right where my grandmother . . .[Lee clarifies this where his great-grandparents lived].

457
00:35:09.363 --> 00:35:12.000
<v ARSENEAULT>To the Mount Zion?

458
00:35:12.000 --> 00:35:13.356
<v LEE>Not Mount Zion.

459
00:35:13.356 --> 00:35:14.712
Mossville Truth Tabernacle.

460
00:35:14.712 --> 00:35:18.780
That’s an old family property that Kim, she donated.

461
00:35:18.780 --> 00:35:23.299
She had got it from Uncle Frederick and Uncle Johnny.

462
00:35:23.299 --> 00:35:32.339
And they all would, they would work back when they was building Maplewood, they would build houses during the day.

463
00:35:32.339 --> 00:35:43.186
And then Uncle Frederick would come home and he would make the cabinets in the cabinet shop on the Old Spanish Trail right there.

464
00:35:43.186 --> 00:35:54.938
And we’d all learn that and, you know, sweep it up and we’d get the shavings out of the shop to put in your horse stall.

465
00:35:54.938 --> 00:35:57.198
You know what I’m saying?

466
00:35:57.198 --> 00:36:00.362
So you had to clean the shop.

467
00:36:00.362 --> 00:36:08.949
And it just was a, it was a cycle there where the family, you’re learning but you’d, you know,

468
00:36:08.949 --> 00:36:17.085
just like a lot of stuff they called me to recycle here [they called him the recycle man].

469
00:36:17.085 --> 00:36:23.412
Well, that fireplace right there that’s in there come out of my mom’s house.

470
00:36:23.412 --> 00:36:24.768
The box itself.

471
00:36:24.768 --> 00:36:28.836
Now I got a man come do the stonework.

472
00:36:28.836 --> 00:36:36.068
And then my board in there, I took, I tore my mom’s house down in Mossville.

473
00:36:36.068 --> 00:36:44.203
And on the inside [of his barn], I go that old cedar, all throughout it [from mother’s house].

474
00:36:44.203 --> 00:36:47.819
And I tore my house down in Mossville.

475
00:36:47.819 --> 00:36:50.983
I built me a camp in Mamou.

476
00:36:50.983 --> 00:36:59.571
And I built like a living quarters, or outdoor kitchen, and my board, from the recycled stuff from Mossville.

477
00:36:59.571 --> 00:37:04.994
I said well man, I say, hey, that’s all it is here.

478
00:37:04.994 --> 00:37:10.418
I don’t, I’ve seen this before, where my uncle’s at, you know?

479
00:37:10.418 --> 00:37:14.938
I said no, that ain’t the way I want it.

480
00:37:14.938 --> 00:37:18.102
They say, “Well, that’s going to leak.”

481
00:37:18.102 --> 00:37:21.718
I say, “No, it ain’t going to leak.”

482
00:37:21.718 --> 00:37:25.333
I say, “You put it on a tilt.

483
00:37:25.333 --> 00:37:37.085
You come up an inch and a half on that corner,” and I said, “and it’s going to pull all that water in, right in that.”

484
00:37:37.085 --> 00:37:41.605
You know and it just, it stems back to Mossville.

485
00:37:41.605 --> 00:37:42.508
You know?

486
00:37:42.508 --> 00:37:44.768
Where you’re bringing the education.

487
00:37:44.768 --> 00:37:52.000
And we don’t have that today in, we’re not sharing our craft with our kids.

488
00:37:52.000 --> 00:38:01.577
And mine were going to a prom one time and my oldest daughter, and I told her, and my son.

489
00:38:01.577 --> 00:38:14.028
I say, “You all got this work to do and you all have it done before the time for you all to go to the prom,

490
00:38:14.028 --> 00:38:16.423
or you all not going.”

491
00:38:16.423 --> 00:38:23.606
<c.p>And man, sure enough, time come up to go, and they didn’t have it ready.

492
00:38:23.606 --> 00:38:26.479
So they, the pace picked up.

493
00:38:26.479 --> 00:38:30.789
The thing is, they said, “Well, Dad, that’s hard.”

494
00:38:30.789 --> 00:38:34.141
I said, “Let me tell you something.

495
00:38:34.141 --> 00:38:36.056
What happened to me.

496
00:38:36.056 --> 00:38:37.972
You think that’s cold.”

497
00:38:37.972 --> 00:38:44.676
I say, “We was helping Uncle Toots [Hartman] put a roof on a house.

498
00:38:44.676 --> 00:38:54.254
Me and Richard, my brother, were packing the shingles up a ladder to put them on top of the house.

499
00:38:54.254 --> 00:38:54.732
Okay?

500
00:38:54.732 --> 00:38:58.085
My class was going on a picnic.

501
00:38:58.085 --> 00:39:00.479
They was going to Galveston.

502
00:39:00.479 --> 00:39:01.915
Mossville High School.

503
00:39:01.915 --> 00:39:04.789
My class, my tenth grade class.

504
00:39:04.789 --> 00:39:12.930
So Daddy told us, “Make sure you all had that on the roof before you leave here.”

505
00:39:12.930 --> 00:39:19.634
So Richard had something to do Friday and so that threw us Saturday morning.

506
00:39:19.634 --> 00:39:27.775
Well Saturday morning, the bus passed down Prater Road, I’m putting shingles on top of the house.

507
00:39:27.775 --> 00:39:28.732
I’m mad.

508
00:39:28.732 --> 00:39:34.000
Talking about my dad under my breath and all that.

509
00:39:34.000 --> 00:39:36.975
<v ARSENEAULT>They were on their way to Texas?

510
00:39:36.975 --> 00:39:39.950
<v LEE>They was on their way to Galveston.

511
00:39:39.950 --> 00:39:40.800
You understand?

512
00:39:40.800 --> 00:39:45.475
So he just said, “You want to go to Galveston, son?”

513
00:39:45.475 --> 00:39:52.275
I said, “Dad, I sure would have liked to have gone with my class to Galveston.”

514
00:39:52.275 --> 00:39:58.225
He said, “Is it with your class, or you want to go to Galveston?”

515
00:39:58.225 --> 00:40:00.775
I say, “Well, both of them.”

516
00:40:00.775 --> 00:40:07.150
He said, “Well,” he said, “one thing about it, you all done finished, all right?”

517
00:40:07.150 --> 00:40:08.850
I said, “Yes, sir.”

518
00:40:08.850 --> 00:40:11.825
He say, “I done call your uncle.”

519
00:40:11.825 --> 00:40:17.350
Papa had a brother, Papa Lemelle had a brother that lived in Galveston.

520
00:40:17.350 --> 00:40:27.125
“And told him that you was going to be late coming down there, but the class was going to be on a bus.

521
00:40:27.125 --> 00:40:33.500
But you see, the thing is, because of the way you worked, and the hustle,

522
00:40:33.500 --> 00:40:44.550
you can get in your car and go to Galveston and meet your class after you have completed the task that I instructed you to do.

523
00:40:44.550 --> 00:40:51.775
But you all finding other things to do, so I’m going to let you go to Galveston.

524
00:40:51.775 --> 00:40:55.600
But you take your car and go to Galveston.”

525
00:40:55.600 --> 00:41:05.375
<c.p>And you know, I said, well, you know, it was a hard lesson, but I didn’t know all the facts at the time.

526
00:41:05.375 --> 00:41:08.775
And he had already took care of that.

527
00:41:08.775 --> 00:41:13.025
Because he knew Richard had a football game Friday night.

528
00:41:13.025 --> 00:41:22.800
And then Friday evening, I wasn’t going to get out there and try to go up and down that ladder with those shingles,

529
00:41:22.800 --> 00:41:26.625
in case something happened, one of us get hurt.

530
00:41:26.625 --> 00:41:29.600
You know, the other one ain’t there.

531
00:41:29.600 --> 00:41:33.425
And that was a, you know, another Mossville lesson.

532
00:41:33.425 --> 00:41:39.800
That you know, you can’t depend, you’ve got to do what you need to do.

533
00:41:39.800 --> 00:41:48.725
And there’s thousands of things like that that happened where you know, you sat around and you’d think about those things.

534
00:41:48.725 --> 00:41:54.675
And you and some of your friends would talk about it, that’s what happened.

535
00:41:54.675 --> 00:41:58.075
And you know, Ms. Martill’s with the canteen.

536
00:41:58.075 --> 00:42:01.050
We used to go hang out there.

537
00:42:01.050 --> 00:42:03.600
And Uncle [Brock?] was a barber.

538
00:42:03.600 --> 00:42:05.300
He cut hair there.

539
00:42:05.300 --> 00:42:07.000
And you know . . .

540
00:42:07.000 --> 00:42:10.220
<v ARSENEAULT>What do you remember about the canteens?

541
00:42:10.220 --> 00:42:10.680
<v LEE>Oh . . .

542
00:42:10.680 --> 00:42:12.519
<v ARSENEAULT>Was there just one?

543
00:42:12.519 --> 00:42:18.039
<v LEE>No. There was, Ms. Lula had one and Ms. Martill had one.

544
00:42:18.039 --> 00:42:20.798
There were three, and Ms. Freelow.

545
00:42:20.798 --> 00:42:21.718
The Freelows.

546
00:42:21.718 --> 00:42:27.237
Cousin Freelow, Cousin Hollis and them had a canteen and fried chicken.

547
00:42:27.237 --> 00:42:28.617
And French fries.

548
00:42:28.617 --> 00:42:30.917
Right down from Mossville School.

549
00:42:30.917 --> 00:42:39.196
And that was the main one there, you know, because you could go there, get some fried chicken.

550
00:42:39.196 --> 00:42:41.496
<v ARSENEAULT>How much did it cost?

551
00:42:41.496 --> 00:42:48.855
<v LEE>I think it was something like fifty-some cents for two pieces of chicken and some fries.

552
00:42:48.855 --> 00:42:52.994
It, man, we had a good time coming up.

553
00:42:52.994 --> 00:42:57.134
And we’d go camping all the time, you know.

554
00:42:57.134 --> 00:43:04.033
Our uncles, you know, hey, November, cold as a, man, we working at the farm.

555
00:43:04.033 --> 00:43:06.792
“You all want to go swimming?”

556
00:43:06.792 --> 00:43:08.172
Somebody said, “Where?

557
00:43:08.172 --> 00:43:14.151
Boy, I sure wish that water…” He said, “Well, hey, build your fire.

558
00:43:14.151 --> 00:43:17.831
If you want to go swimming, go swimming.”

559
00:43:17.831 --> 00:43:18.751
You know?

560
00:43:18.751 --> 00:43:26.110
But prepare yourself for when you get out of there, how cold it’s going to be.

561
00:43:26.110 --> 00:43:30.709
So you build your fire before you go in there.

562
00:43:30.709 --> 00:43:33.009
You know what I’m saying?

563
00:43:33.009 --> 00:43:38.988
He said or either I’ll crank the truck and put the heater on.

564
00:43:38.988 --> 00:43:43.128
<v ARSENEAULT>So you get out and warm up really quick.

565
00:43:43.128 --> 00:43:43.588
<v LEE>Yeah.

566
00:43:43.588 --> 00:43:48.647
You get out, when you come out of there, you’re through.

567
00:43:48.647 --> 00:43:50.947
You ain’t going back in.

568
00:43:50.947 --> 00:43:54.166
You’re going to swim all you want.

569
00:43:54.166 --> 00:44:02.905
He says, “It’s going to be cold, but it’s not going to be that cold for in and out.”

570
00:44:02.905 --> 00:44:10.724
He said, “If you get in there, stay in there till you get ready to get out.

571
00:44:10.724 --> 00:44:11.644
You know?

572
00:44:11.644 --> 00:44:15.323
But prepare yourself for when you get out.

573
00:44:15.323 --> 00:44:17.163
You build a fire.”

574
00:44:17.163 --> 00:44:20.843
So we was up there in the woods.

575
00:44:20.843 --> 00:44:28.202
We went and got a bunch of them old pine knots and built a big fire.

576
00:44:28.202 --> 00:44:32.801
And just, you know, you got to do boy things.

577
00:44:32.801 --> 00:44:36.021
We had a pond, a dynamite hole.

578
00:44:36.021 --> 00:44:42.000
And we’d take a number three tub and we called it skiing.

579
00:44:42.000 --> 00:44:43.835
<v ARSENEAULT>What’s a number three tub?

580
00:44:43.835 --> 00:44:45.303
<v LEE>You know, a washtub.

581
00:44:45.303 --> 00:44:46.037
You know?

582
00:44:46.037 --> 00:44:47.505
<v ARSENEAULT>Like a metal tub?

583
00:44:47.505 --> 00:44:48.606
<v LEE>Yeah, metal tub.

584
00:44:48.606 --> 00:44:50.440
I got one out there.

585
00:44:50.440 --> 00:44:51.541
<v ARSENEAULT>For the horses?

586
00:44:51.541 --> 00:44:53.743
<v LEE>No. You see it back there?

587
00:44:53.743 --> 00:44:55.945
People used to wash in those.

588
00:44:55.945 --> 00:44:59.615
Look under that stainless steel table, right by the bricks.

589
00:44:59.615 --> 00:45:00.349
You see?

590
00:45:00.349 --> 00:45:01.450
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, I see.

591
00:45:01.450 --> 00:45:02.550
It’s propped up.

592
00:45:02.550 --> 00:45:04.018
<v LEE>Yeah, it’s propped up.

593
00:45:04.018 --> 00:45:05.486
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah, I see it.

594
00:45:05.486 --> 00:45:05.853
Yeah.

595
00:45:05.853 --> 00:45:10.624
<v LEE>We’d take a rope and get on the other side of the pond.

596
00:45:10.624 --> 00:45:16.495
And have a rope and tie it on the pommel of the saddle of the horse.

597
00:45:16.495 --> 00:45:17.963
And pull it across.

598
00:45:17.963 --> 00:45:22.000
Take off with the horse. (laughs) It may be skiing.

599
00:45:22.000 --> 00:45:24.857
<v ARSENEAULT>What happened when you fall out?

600
00:45:24.857 --> 00:45:28.667
<v LEE>Well, you’d be in the water, you know?

601
00:45:28.667 --> 00:45:39.143
But nine out of ten, if the horse running fast enough, you come on across the pond in the tub, you know?

602
00:45:39.143 --> 00:45:43.905
(Arseneault laughs) So that’s fun things that we would do.

603
00:45:43.905 --> 00:45:51.048
It’s like the carwash, back then, you know, we didn’t have the big city water.

604
00:45:51.048 --> 00:45:54.857
Everybody had a well and stuff, you know?

605
00:45:54.857 --> 00:46:04.381
And you could drive back to the dynamite hole in the car and go back there and wash the cars.

606
00:46:04.381 --> 00:46:15.810
And you know, we didn’t have what you’d call running water because we had a pump, we had the hand pump sort of thing.

607
00:46:15.810 --> 00:46:25.810
And then when the water thing came, we had, Papa and them dug a well, Mama and them dug a well.

608
00:46:25.810 --> 00:46:27.714
We had running water.

609
00:46:27.714 --> 00:46:32.000
But for years there, we didn’t have running water.

610
00:46:32.000 --> 00:46:32.952
You know?

611
00:46:32.952 --> 00:46:35.333
Because I’m 67 years old.

612
00:46:35.333 --> 00:46:38.190
And we didn’t have running water.

613
00:46:38.190 --> 00:46:41.524
And then the water system came through.

614
00:46:41.524 --> 00:46:50.095
When the water system came through, I was a grown man, because I served on the water board.

615
00:46:50.095 --> 00:46:52.000
Mossville water district.

616
00:46:52.000 --> 00:46:54.194
<v ARSENEAULT>How did that happen?

617
00:46:54.194 --> 00:47:07.359
<v LEE>Through the health, the board, through the health unit and what do you call this program, through a federal grant that we applied for.

618
00:47:07.359 --> 00:47:10.102
And we got the money.

619
00:47:10.102 --> 00:47:11.199
This organization.

620
00:47:11.199 --> 00:47:16.684
I can’t call the name of it at this time.

621
00:47:16.684 --> 00:47:32.044
But we got a federal grant and put in the water system because it was a lot going on during those times about the purification of the water,

622
00:47:32.044 --> 00:47:35.883
and the water table was being polluted.

623
00:47:35.883 --> 00:47:43.015
And they wanted to get away from the shallow wells that we had.

624
00:47:43.015 --> 00:47:47.951
And a 100-foot well was considered a shallow well.

625
00:47:47.951 --> 00:47:58.374
And some people in the neighborhood, we had a 200-foot well where they would service more than one house.

626
00:47:58.374 --> 00:48:10.990
And the water company and the federal government and the board of health, because, came about with a grant and everything to build

627
00:48:10.990 --> 00:48:15.927
<c.p>water systems in undeveloped neighborhoods and stuff like that.

628
00:48:15.927 --> 00:48:18.121
Because we wasn’t incorporated.

629
00:48:18.121 --> 00:48:21.961
And that was one of the things.

630
00:48:21.961 --> 00:48:26.350
I served eight years on the water board.

631
00:48:26.350 --> 00:48:33.481
I wasn’t on the original water board, but some of my friends were.

632
00:48:33.481 --> 00:48:45.000
And I took one of them’s place on the water board, and then I stayed on it for eight years.

633
00:48:45.000 --> 00:48:47.769
<v ARSENEAULT>Was it all Mossville residents?

634
00:48:47.769 --> 00:48:48.323
<v LEE>Yeah.

635
00:48:48.323 --> 00:48:49.984
All Mossville residents.

636
00:48:49.984 --> 00:48:56.630
And then later on they tied it into, I think to Sulphur.

637
00:48:56.630 --> 00:49:11.028
And just in case we had an emergency, our well would go out or the Maplewood well would go out, and that we’d have a backup.

638
00:49:11.028 --> 00:49:14.905
And the same thing with the sewage.

639
00:49:14.905 --> 00:49:22.104
We got a federal grant came through and did the sewage in Mossville.

640
00:49:22.104 --> 00:49:24.320
And that was great.

641
00:49:24.320 --> 00:49:29.304
And you know, [unclear] It really, I enjoyed it.

642
00:49:29.304 --> 00:49:33.180
I couldn’t imagine growing up anywhere else.

643
00:49:33.180 --> 00:49:39.826
And we experienced the things that I had a chance to do.

644
00:49:39.826 --> 00:49:48.133
And you know, because like hey, hell, when I came up, the typewriter was amazing.

645
00:49:48.133 --> 00:49:50.902
You know what I’m saying?

646
00:49:50.902 --> 00:49:59.209
And now, all you’ve got to do is talk and it records and print out.

647
00:49:59.209 --> 00:50:03.639
And I’ve seen technology just – (whistles) You know?

648
00:50:03.639 --> 00:50:06.408
And the TV, you know.

649
00:50:06.408 --> 00:50:13.054
Mossville, we had a TV and my grandmother and them had one.

650
00:50:13.054 --> 00:50:20.253
And then, you know, people just come to your house and watch TV.

651
00:50:20.253 --> 00:50:32.991
Your friends, especially on Saturdays, if we wasn’t going to the farm, we’d get up and several friends come over, watch the cartoons.

652
00:50:32.991 --> 00:50:36.867
The Three Stooges and stuff like that.

653
00:50:36.867 --> 00:50:42.959
And if I had a TV, my friends had a TV.

654
00:50:42.959 --> 00:50:49.051
If I had a car, they could ride, too, you know?

655
00:50:49.051 --> 00:50:55.142
But Daddy said, don’t let them drive, but they could ride.

656
00:50:55.142 --> 00:50:56.250
You know?

657
00:50:56.250 --> 00:51:00.127
Because they ain’t got no driver’s license.

658
00:51:00.127 --> 00:51:01.234
You know?

659
00:51:01.234 --> 00:51:07.326
Don’t ride too many, and don’t ride nobody you don’t know.

660
00:51:07.326 --> 00:51:09.541
If they not one

661
00:51:09.541 --> 00:51:18.956
<c.p>of your associates, no. Because they talk to, you know, we was all, it was all family.

662
00:51:18.956 --> 00:51:25.047
It was the same thing when my cousin got a car.

663
00:51:25.047 --> 00:51:32.801
I couldn't wait to go to college and try to better myself, you know?

664
00:51:32.801 --> 00:51:36.123
Like the neighborhood was behind me.

665
00:51:36.123 --> 00:51:40.000
I’d go to school in Mossville . . .

666
00:51:40.000 --> 00:51:42.567
<v ARSENEAULT>You mean they were supporting you?

667
00:51:42.567 --> 00:51:42.995
<v LEE>[Pardon?]?

668
00:51:42.995 --> 00:51:47.274
<v ARSENEAULT>You mean they’re behind you like they were supporting you?

669
00:51:47.274 --> 00:51:49.841
<v LEE>Supporting you, any kind of way.

670
00:51:49.841 --> 00:51:50.697
Spiritually, financially.

671
00:51:50.697 --> 00:51:56.688
Not that much, you know, they give you five dollars, two dollars, three dollars.

672
00:51:56.688 --> 00:52:06.101
You know, we had just like, we had a couple local people we called, what, we’d call them a wine head today.

673
00:52:06.101 --> 00:52:08.240
You know what I’m saying?

674
00:52:08.240 --> 00:52:12.947
And they would see you, and you’d be home from school.

675
00:52:12.947 --> 00:52:18.510
We’d went and played a game somewhere at a high school or something.

676
00:52:18.510 --> 00:52:20.221
“Hey, boy, come here!

677
00:52:20.221 --> 00:52:21.077
Come here!”

678
00:52:21.077 --> 00:52:28.779
And then they’d call you over and give you, “Look, man, I sure don’t want to do this,

679
00:52:28.779 --> 00:52:35.197
but yeah, man, you and your boys, you all played a good game last night.

680
00:52:35.197 --> 00:52:37.764
Go buy you all a soda.”

681
00:52:37.764 --> 00:52:43.327
You could get a soda for a dime back then, a nickel, really.

682
00:52:43.327 --> 00:52:49.317
You know, they’d give you a quarter, 50 cents, you know, a little help.

683
00:52:49.317 --> 00:52:51.029
“You all keep winning.

684
00:52:51.029 --> 00:52:53.168
Keep Mossville on the map.

685
00:52:53.168 --> 00:52:55.308
You know what I’m saying?”

686
00:52:55.308 --> 00:52:58.303
Everybody used to say, “Where is Mossville?”

687
00:52:58.303 --> 00:53:03.010
But then after a while, Mossville name became a household item.

688
00:53:03.010 --> 00:53:07.288
Because one year we won state football, basketball and track.

689
00:53:07.288 --> 00:53:09.000
The same year.

690
00:53:09.000 --> 00:53:10.889
<v ARSENEAULT>What year was that?

691
00:53:10.889 --> 00:53:12.306
<v LEE>In the ‘60s.

692
00:53:12.306 --> 00:53:15.139
That was in the early 60s.

693
00:53:15.139 --> 00:53:18.444
And we had a high school all-American.

694
00:53:18.444 --> 00:53:19.389
Johnny Comeaux.

695
00:53:19.389 --> 00:53:24.111
And Johnny averaged 34 points a game in high school.

696
00:53:24.111 --> 00:53:26.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Was that basketball?

697
00:53:26.000 --> 00:53:26.466
<v LEE>Basketball.

698
00:53:26.466 --> 00:53:37.659
And we had a, and they had, football team won state that same year, they had seven sets of brothers on the football team.

699
00:53:37.659 --> 00:53:39.058
You know, Mossville.

700
00:53:39.058 --> 00:53:44.188
Hey, the trophy was so big, it’s coming from the playoff,

701
00:53:44.188 --> 00:53:53.049
they had to stop and put the trainer on the bus with the trophy because we went in cars.

702
00:53:53.049 --> 00:53:55.848
We didn’t go on the bus.

703
00:53:55.848 --> 00:53:58.646
<v ARSENEAULT>It didn’t fit in anybody’s car?

704
00:53:58.646 --> 00:54:01.444
<v LEE>It wouldn’t fit in anybody’s car.

705
00:54:01.444 --> 00:54:06.574
We were all smacked in there, you know what I’m saying?

706
00:54:06.574 --> 00:54:08.439
But we won it.

707
00:54:08.439 --> 00:54:11.704
Had the trophy hanging out the window.

708
00:54:11.704 --> 00:54:15.901
And it was cold coming from up around [Weymouth?

709
00:54:15.901 --> 00:54:17.767
Lee clarifies: North Louisiana].

710
00:54:17.767 --> 00:54:20.099
And they had to stop.

711
00:54:20.099 --> 00:54:23.363
Of course they know, it was cold.

712
00:54:23.363 --> 00:54:28.960
They say oh, no, we’re going to stop and put somebody on.

713
00:54:28.960 --> 00:54:35.489
I think it was Buford Garrett they put on the bus with the trophy.

714
00:54:35.489 --> 00:54:43.417
Because we had no room in the car for him with the equipment and all the players.

715
00:54:43.417 --> 00:54:49.013
And it made us that much tighter, you know, as a community.

716
00:54:49.013 --> 00:54:53.211
And the people really seemed like they appreciated it.

717
00:54:53.211 --> 00:54:56.942
Oh, Ms. Payne cooking something tonight for everybody.

718
00:54:56.942 --> 00:54:59.740
Or Ms. Delafosse is cooking supper.

719
00:54:59.740 --> 00:55:10.000
You know, if you be somewhere at someone’s house and they cooking, hey, especially Big Mama, we called her Big Mama.

720
00:55:10.000 --> 00:55:11.349
<v ARSENEAULT>That’s Ms. Julia’s mother?

721
00:55:11.349 --> 00:55:11.687
<v LEE>Yeah.

722
00:55:11.687 --> 00:55:12.361
Big Mama.

723
00:55:12.361 --> 00:55:13.036
Everybody, man . . .

724
00:55:13.036 --> 00:55:17.759
<v ARSENEAULT>Did she live in the house on the Old Spanish Trail with the window?

725
00:55:17.759 --> 00:55:18.096
<v LEE>Yeah.

726
00:55:18.096 --> 00:55:19.108
Yeah, with the . . .

727
00:55:19.108 --> 00:55:20.458
<v ARSENEAULT>With the one window?

728
00:55:20.458 --> 00:55:20.795
<v LEE>Yeah.

729
00:55:20.795 --> 00:55:22.482
The house is still there.

730
00:55:22.482 --> 00:55:24.506
<v ARSENEAULT>So that was Big Mama’s house?

731
00:55:24.506 --> 00:55:25.855
<v LEE>That was Big Mama.

732
00:55:25.855 --> 00:55:26.193
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

733
00:55:26.193 --> 00:55:26.867
Got it.

734
00:55:26.867 --> 00:55:33.277
<v LEE>See, my grandfather owned that whole block, all the way down to where them Praters lived at down there.

735
00:55:33.277 --> 00:55:34.289
<v ARSENEAULT>Jim Lee did?

736
00:55:34.289 --> 00:55:34.627
<v LEE>No.

737
00:55:34.627 --> 00:55:34.964
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

738
00:55:34.964 --> 00:55:35.639
<v LEE>Edward Lemelle.

739
00:55:35.639 --> 00:55:36.313
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay, okay.

740
00:55:36.313 --> 00:55:37.325
<v LEE>Butch [unclear] Lemelle.

741
00:55:37.325 --> 00:55:38.000
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

742
00:55:38.000 --> 00:55:47.021
<v LEE>Jim Lee owned from Highway 90, going back to Bayou D’Inde, back there by PPG.

743
00:55:47.021 --> 00:55:48.224
<v ARSENEAULT>That’s right.

744
00:55:48.224 --> 00:55:48.825
Okay.

745
00:55:48.825 --> 00:55:53.636
<v LEE>And Edward Lemelle owned that, and Big Mama.

746
00:55:53.636 --> 00:55:57.846
And Grandpa always made a big garden.

747
00:55:57.846 --> 00:56:01.455
And we all worked the garden.

748
00:56:01.455 --> 00:56:05.664
And he had some animals, barnyard animals.

749
00:56:05.664 --> 00:56:17.091
And there was always something cooking at Big Mama’s. [unclear] or some toast or something like that, you know.

750
00:56:17.091 --> 00:56:21.301
If you were there, you eat, too.

751
00:56:21.301 --> 00:56:29.720
That’s why everybody in the family would go after school, especially, my mother worked.

752
00:56:29.720 --> 00:56:37.538
Shelton, her mother worked, and Jerry [unclear], we’d all go to Big Mama’s.

753
00:56:37.538 --> 00:56:42.350
That was the babysitter, the chastiser, and everything.

754
00:56:42.350 --> 00:56:47.161
And every Sunday, Big Mama’s house for dinner.

755
00:56:47.161 --> 00:56:51.371
<v ARSENEAULT>What kinds of things would she cook?

756
00:56:51.371 --> 00:56:55.580
<v LEE>Oh, man, fried chicken and peach pie.

757
00:56:55.580 --> 00:56:57.385
You name it.

758
00:56:57.385 --> 00:56:59.790
Pork ribs and gumbo.

759
00:56:59.790 --> 00:57:04.000
Man, man, Big Mama could cook.

760
00:57:04.000 --> 00:57:08.156
<v ARSENEAULT>Did you have a favorite thing that she would make?

761
00:57:08.156 --> 00:57:10.234
<v LEE>Peach cobbler and [sweet] cornbread.

762
00:57:10.234 --> 00:57:12.727
I love my cornbread and milk.

763
00:57:12.727 --> 00:57:13.558
You know?

764
00:57:13.558 --> 00:57:14.805
It wasn’t [unclear].

765
00:57:14.805 --> 00:57:16.883
It was cornbread and milk.

766
00:57:16.883 --> 00:57:18.130
I liked it.

767
00:57:18.130 --> 00:57:25.195
And [unclear] Big Mama, and she could . . .and you’d go pick blackberries, and she had blackberry preserves.

768
00:57:25.195 --> 00:57:27.273
All the mayhaws, mayhaw preserve.

769
00:57:27.273 --> 00:57:32.260
That’s why I planted all the fruit trees I’ve got out here.

770
00:57:32.260 --> 00:57:34.753
Pears and mayhaws and all that.

771
00:57:34.753 --> 00:57:37.662
We can that for food, you know?

772
00:57:37.662 --> 00:57:46.390
And, but she used to, and she’d do, and then during the winter, there was a big crowd coming through there.

773
00:57:46.390 --> 00:57:50.545
And we’d eat and she always had something to cook.

774
00:57:50.545 --> 00:57:55.117
And my grandfather would always say, “Get out of my icebox!

775
00:57:55.117 --> 00:57:57.610
Get out of my icebox, boy!

776
00:57:57.610 --> 00:57:59.273
Just hold the door.

777
00:57:59.273 --> 00:58:03.844
Go in there and get you what you need out there.

778
00:58:03.844 --> 00:58:08.000
You know what you want before you go in there.

779
00:58:08.000 --> 00:58:10.078
You want something to eat.

780
00:58:10.078 --> 00:58:14.649
So get something to eat and get out of that icebox!”

781
00:58:14.649 --> 00:58:17.974
He’d just be messing with us, you know.

782
00:58:17.974 --> 00:58:22.545
I said, “Oh, Papa, you need me to cook you something?”

783
00:58:22.545 --> 00:58:27.532
I said, “No, I’m just looking to see if that guy left.”

784
00:58:27.532 --> 00:58:29.610
He said, “What you want?

785
00:58:29.610 --> 00:58:31.273
I’ll fix you something.

786
00:58:31.273 --> 00:58:32.935
I’ll fix you something.”

787
00:58:32.935 --> 00:58:35.013
He had us all spoiled.

788
00:58:35.013 --> 00:58:39.584
But he talked like he was going to beat you down.

789
00:58:39.584 --> 00:58:41.247
Ain’t never whipped me.

790
00:58:41.247 --> 00:58:49.558
That’s the only person in the family didn’t whip me. (laughs) My aunty, all my aunties, and my uncles and

791
00:58:49.558 --> 00:58:50.390
<c.p>all them.

792
00:58:50.390 --> 00:58:54.961
I call it, no, Kim never did and Mama never did.

793
00:58:54.961 --> 00:58:56.623
Never did whip me.

794
00:58:56.623 --> 00:59:07.429
But for the rest of them, my great aunts and all of them, and my grandfather, he never did whip me, Papa never did whip me.

795
00:59:07.429 --> 00:59:11.584
But the rest of them got [a part of me?].

796
00:59:11.584 --> 00:59:14.494
And Kim, Kim and my Aunt Judy.

797
00:59:14.494 --> 00:59:19.896
Because she, man, she the one taught us how to play football, really.

798
00:59:19.896 --> 00:59:23.221
Every [June?] we’re kicking Kim [and her mama?].

799
00:59:23.221 --> 00:59:24.883
Yeah, she was young.

800
00:59:24.883 --> 00:59:26.961
And we was coming up.

801
00:59:26.961 --> 00:59:34.026
And me and her would play, we’d play Butch, Richard and Bubba would be on one team.

802
00:59:34.026 --> 00:59:38.597
And them me and Aunt Judy were building a team together.

803
00:59:38.597 --> 00:59:41.922
She said, “I’m going to block for you.

804
00:59:41.922 --> 00:59:44.000
You run the ball.”

805
00:59:44.000 --> 00:59:46.612
<v ARSENEAULT>So she was a tomboy.

806
00:59:46.612 --> 00:59:47.135
<v LEE>Oh!

807
00:59:47.135 --> 00:59:48.702
Was she ever.

808
00:59:48.702 --> 00:59:50.269
Was she ever!

809
00:59:50.269 --> 00:59:53.926
She could play, and she was rough.

810
00:59:53.926 --> 00:59:59.673
Aunt Julia, Aunt Julia, I got a lot of respect for.

811
00:59:59.673 --> 01:00:06.465
Because after her husband died, Aunt Julia went to work in the plant.

812
01:00:06.465 --> 01:00:10.122
And worked like a man, you know?

813
01:00:10.122 --> 01:00:14.301
I have a lot of respect for her.

814
01:00:14.301 --> 01:00:25.795
My brother tried to get [her to go to college], between us, he tried to get her to go back to school.

815
01:00:25.795 --> 01:00:29.452
Because she was smart and educational, book-wise.

816
01:00:29.452 --> 01:00:33.109
And she said, “I got these kids.

817
01:00:33.109 --> 01:00:36.244
I got to go to work.”

818
01:00:36.244 --> 01:00:42.513
She said, “I’m not going to sit here and wait on welfare.”

819
01:00:42.513 --> 01:00:49.304
She put her boots on and she went to work in the plant.

820
01:00:49.304 --> 01:00:53.484
And worked in the plants a long time.

821
01:00:53.484 --> 01:00:56.096
And paid for her house.

822
01:00:56.096 --> 01:00:58.708
I admire her for that.

823
01:00:58.708 --> 01:01:06.022
She talks some smack, and every one time, she can back it up, too.

824
01:01:06.022 --> 01:01:07.067
Aunt Julia.

825
01:01:07.067 --> 01:01:11.769
And the stories there, and everything, Kim’s dad, too.

826
01:01:11.769 --> 01:01:14.904
He would make us work hard.

827
01:01:14.904 --> 01:01:23.263
You know, he’d say, “If you was a college professor, you wouldn’t have to do this.

828
01:01:23.263 --> 01:01:25.875
But you a college professor?”

829
01:01:25.875 --> 01:01:30.054
“No.” “Well, get your ass on over there.

830
01:01:30.054 --> 01:01:32.144
Get on that shovel.”

831
01:01:32.144 --> 01:01:35.279
How you dare to say that?

832
01:01:35.279 --> 01:01:38.936
“I’m educating you about ignorance,” he said.

833
01:01:38.936 --> 01:01:40.503
“I’m helping, too.”

834
01:01:40.503 --> 01:01:45.205
Say, “You don’t have to do that, Uncle [unclear].”

835
01:01:45.205 --> 01:01:47.295
Picked it up, man.

836
01:01:47.295 --> 01:01:51.997
And that was the thing back in the ‘60s.

837
01:01:51.997 --> 01:01:54.087
A lot of us

838
01:01:54.087 --> 01:02:00.356
<c.p>did real good, you know, that my crowd there, my age group.

839
01:02:00.356 --> 01:02:04.535
We turned out real good there for Mossville.

840
01:02:04.535 --> 01:02:09.237
And some got some doctors and lawyers from Mossville.

841
01:02:09.237 --> 01:02:17.596
And, you know, my kids were among the group that Mossville [achieved in getting an education].

842
01:02:17.596 --> 01:02:19.163
And Mossville, Westlake.

843
01:02:19.163 --> 01:02:27.000
I don't know if there anything else you, I’ve been rambling on, you know.

844
01:02:27.000 --> 01:02:31.113
<v ARSENEAULT>I wanted to ask you about the farm.

845
01:02:31.113 --> 01:02:35.226
What kind of things did you all grow?

846
01:02:35.226 --> 01:02:38.825
So you, Edward Lemelle had a garden.

847
01:02:38.825 --> 01:02:39.854
<v LEE>Oh, yeah.

848
01:02:39.854 --> 01:02:43.967
<v ARSENEAULT>And then your other grandfather had a farm?

849
01:02:43.967 --> 01:02:51.679
<v LEE>My other grandfather, he, well, first of all, to answer your first question, Edward Lemelle,

850
01:02:51.679 --> 01:02:59.906
we planted like corn, okra, field peas, snap beans, Irish potatoes, you know, and mustard greens.

851
01:02:59.906 --> 01:03:00.420
Cabbage.

852
01:03:00.420 --> 01:03:07.104
Whatever time of the year it was for growing this, we had that.

853
01:03:07.104 --> 01:03:08.132
You know?

854
01:03:08.132 --> 01:03:14.302
And you’d break this and you’d turn it up, and plant here.

855
01:03:14.302 --> 01:03:20.472
Old Charlie, they would plow, an old horse, he had a plow.

856
01:03:20.472 --> 01:03:22.528
He had no tractor.

857
01:03:22.528 --> 01:03:27.670
<v ARSENEAULT>Was that behind the house on the Old Spanish Trail?

858
01:03:27.670 --> 01:03:31.269
<v LEE>Behind the house on Old Spanish Trail.

859
01:03:31.269 --> 01:03:34.868
One side of it was the garden.

860
01:03:34.868 --> 01:03:42.580
And on the other side, he had the chicken yards and some pigs, pig pens.

861
01:03:42.580 --> 01:03:45.151
And pear trees, fig trees.

862
01:03:45.151 --> 01:03:49.264
And but Grandpa again, he had like cows.

863
01:03:49.264 --> 01:03:54.920
He didn’t raise too much of anything, the more than Grandma

864
01:03:54.920 --> 01:03:58.519
<c.p>had, like a black pepper tree bush.

865
01:03:58.519 --> 01:04:01.604
And she had like green onions.

866
01:04:01.604 --> 01:04:07.259
And she had a little patch right outside the back there.

867
01:04:07.259 --> 01:04:14.458
But so for like the big garden, no. Because Grandpa Jim had the farm.

868
01:04:14.458 --> 01:04:16.000
He had . . .

869
01:04:16.000 --> 01:04:16.477
<v ARSENEAULT>Livestock.

870
01:04:16.477 --> 01:04:16.955
<v LEE>Yeah.

871
01:04:16.955 --> 01:04:17.432
Livestock.

872
01:04:17.432 --> 01:04:19.819
And then he had trucks.

873
01:04:19.819 --> 01:04:21.728
He had two trucks.

874
01:04:21.728 --> 01:04:28.889
He would take them in, my daddy’s brother, and his cousin, would drive them in,

875
01:04:28.889 --> 01:04:36.049
go through Mossville, pick up a lot of the people that worked at the docks.

876
01:04:36.049 --> 01:04:37.959
Put them to work.

877
01:04:37.959 --> 01:04:45.597
And when Joyce Therioux and Steadmans, he would go, he would send my uncle and them,

878
01:04:45.597 --> 01:04:51.325
would go pick up the produce that they’d [wanted] to throw away.

879
01:04:51.325 --> 01:04:56.099
And they would bring that over there for the animals.

880
01:04:56.099 --> 01:04:59.918
And you know, that’s what he would do.

881
01:04:59.918 --> 01:05:04.691
And my uncle and Mr. Siase would drive the trucks.

882
01:05:04.691 --> 01:05:16.148
In the afternoon, they’d clean the trucks back up and go back and pick the men up from the waterfront and take them home.

883
01:05:16.148 --> 01:05:22.354
And that, and then Grandpa would clean up and go to the drive-in.

884
01:05:22.354 --> 01:05:27.128
To the, and work the drive-in, to the picture show.

885
01:05:27.128 --> 01:05:29.514
And that’s what he did.

886
01:05:29.514 --> 01:05:30.469
<v ARSENEAULT>At night.

887
01:05:30.469 --> 01:05:31.424
<v LEE>At night.

888
01:05:31.424 --> 01:05:34.765
And Papa Lemelle had a big garden.

889
01:05:34.765 --> 01:05:38.107
And Uncle Shelton ran Dr. Fellow’s farm.

890
01:05:38.107 --> 01:05:43.358
And hay, sweet potatoes, peanuts and Irish potatoes, stuff like that.

891
01:05:43.358 --> 01:05:46.700
That’s what they would plant out there.

892
01:05:46.700 --> 01:05:57.679
And it all came together, you know, every now and then Grandpa Jim, if they had a bust sack of rice or something,

893
01:05:57.679 --> 01:06:04.362
they could get it and buy it or we got food from the docks.

894
01:06:04.362 --> 01:06:05.794
You know, beans.

895
01:06:05.794 --> 01:06:08.181
The same thing with beans.

896
01:06:08.181 --> 01:06:12.955
The bag would bust and they’d have a little left.

897
01:06:12.955 --> 01:06:13.909
And it

898
01:06:13.909 --> 01:06:23.934
<c.p>would just survival, you know, and when Grandpa would divide it up, you know, “Hey, Saise, you can have that today.

899
01:06:23.934 --> 01:06:28.708
Bob, take that, give that to Ms. Rembert down there.”

900
01:06:28.708 --> 01:06:34.914
You know, some of the old people in the community that they shared.

901
01:06:34.914 --> 01:06:35.868
You know?

902
01:06:35.868 --> 01:06:46.848
And, you know, because sometime you come home and they got a big old piece of meat there, and where it come from?

903
01:06:46.848 --> 01:06:54.008
Well, Mr. Braxton and them barbecued, they killed a calf, or they kill a hog.

904
01:06:54.008 --> 01:07:02.601
And Daddy made me and Richard go down there and help them so they give us some meat.

905
01:07:02.601 --> 01:07:06.897
And what are we going to do, bring it?

906
01:07:06.897 --> 01:07:07.374
Yeah.

907
01:07:07.374 --> 01:07:10.716
You know, for Big Mama to cook.

908
01:07:10.716 --> 01:07:13.580
“Bring that to your grandma, boy.”

909
01:07:13.580 --> 01:07:15.967
You know, stuff like that.

910
01:07:15.967 --> 01:07:24.560
And at the time, we just, oh, man, I got to go down here and scrape this hog.

911
01:07:24.560 --> 01:07:27.424
I got to go kill it.”

912
01:07:27.424 --> 01:07:34.584
And we, off the record, this past weekend, we was on that ride last week.

913
01:07:34.584 --> 01:07:38.403
And we was out there in the country.

914
01:07:38.403 --> 01:07:42.700
And a man said, “You all want a hog?”

915
01:07:42.700 --> 01:07:50.815
And [Bobby?] says, “Yeah, we want one, but we got to take him to the slaughter house.”

916
01:07:50.815 --> 01:07:55.588
I said, “Man, no. We can kill him right here.

917
01:07:55.588 --> 01:07:58.930
We ain’t got to do all that.”

918
01:07:58.930 --> 01:08:05.136
A couple of them old boys, they knew how to do it, too.

919
01:08:05.136 --> 01:08:08.000
But we butchered right there.

920
01:08:08.000 --> 01:08:09.635
<v ARSENEAULT>Had a pig roast.

921
01:08:09.635 --> 01:08:10.043
<v LEE>Yeah.

922
01:08:10.043 --> 01:08:13.721
Had a hog, we had a couchon de lait.

923
01:08:13.721 --> 01:08:18.624
And cut that back strap out of it and had backbone [stew].

924
01:08:18.624 --> 01:08:21.893
And then [while couchon de lait was cooking].

925
01:08:21.893 --> 01:08:23.528
Over there, you know.

926
01:08:23.528 --> 01:08:27.206
<v ARSENEAULT>You wouldn’t have known how to do that otherwise.

927
01:08:27.206 --> 01:08:27.614
<v LEE>Yeah.

928
01:08:27.614 --> 01:08:32.109
“It’s 2015, man, what do you know about killing a hog?”

929
01:08:32.109 --> 01:08:36.195
<c.p>I said, “Man, you’d be surprised the things I know.

930
01:08:36.195 --> 01:08:38.647
Having been around all my life.”

931
01:08:38.647 --> 01:08:43.551
I said, “Man, I ain’t no forty-some years old, no city slicker.

932
01:08:43.551 --> 01:08:45.594
I come from the country.

933
01:08:45.594 --> 01:08:46.411
You know?”

934
01:08:46.411 --> 01:08:49.272
I say, I come from hard times.

935
01:08:49.272 --> 01:08:50.906
Not really hard times.

936
01:08:50.906 --> 01:08:54.175
But times, the way things was, you know?

937
01:08:54.175 --> 01:08:57.036
And I contribute that back to Mossville.

938
01:08:57.036 --> 01:08:57.444
Yeah.

939
01:08:57.444 --> 01:09:03.165
And shit, we got a saw, man, and went to work on that thing.

940
01:09:03.165 --> 01:09:05.208
“We ain’t got no tools!”

941
01:09:05.208 --> 01:09:08.886
I say, “You got a hardware store up there.”

942
01:09:08.886 --> 01:09:13.789
I say, “We can get a saw for about ten, fifteen dollars.”

943
01:09:13.789 --> 01:09:15.015
“Oh, man, [unclear].”

944
01:09:15.015 --> 01:09:18.693
I say, “No, you’re going to do the work.

945
01:09:18.693 --> 01:09:22.371
I’m going to tell you how to do it.”

946
01:09:22.371 --> 01:09:30.135
And then the [one boy?] say, “Yeah, I know how to clean them and everything, but to cut them?”

947
01:09:30.135 --> 01:09:39.124
I say, “We’re going to cut them, we’re going to cut them, cure and clean them and take that back strap out.

948
01:09:39.124 --> 01:09:44.028
And we’re going to make a sauce out of that back strap.

949
01:09:44.028 --> 01:09:49.749
And we’re going to keep that hog, and we’re going to roast that hog.”

950
01:09:49.749 --> 01:10:00.782
Because one of the boys had a pit that we put half of that hog on one side and half on the other side in a rotisserie.

951
01:10:00.782 --> 01:10:08.137
And I say, shit, or we can dig a hole in it and wrap them, coon ass microwave.

952
01:10:08.137 --> 01:10:09.772
He say, “Country boy.”

953
01:10:09.772 --> 01:10:13.041
I say, “That’s what I’m talking about, buddy.

954
01:10:13.041 --> 01:10:15.084
That’s what I’m talking about.”

955
01:10:15.084 --> 01:10:20.805
And it all comes from being exposed to it in childhood there in Mossville.

956
01:10:20.805 --> 01:10:29.386
The way a lot of the kids closer to Lake Charles than Westlake was knew nothing of that kind of life.

957
01:10:29.386 --> 01:10:31.429
And the survival, you know.

958
01:10:31.429 --> 01:10:32.655
During the hurricane.

959
01:10:32.655 --> 01:10:33.472
You know?

960
01:10:33.472 --> 01:10:37.967
And I thought about the things like my grandfather and them.

961
01:10:37.967 --> 01:10:39.193
Your chicken yard.

962
01:10:39.193 --> 01:10:43.279
He had some chickens he kept up off the floor.

963
01:10:43.279 --> 01:10:46.548
And he said they was on death row.

964
01:10:46.548 --> 01:10:49.000
My grandfather used to say.

965
01:10:49.000 --> 01:10:51.606
<v ARSENEAULT>Chickens were on death row?

966
01:10:51.606 --> 01:10:52.127
<v LEE>Yeah.

967
01:10:52.127 --> 01:11:03.593
You put them up off the ground in a pen, and you feed them the corn, you’re getting them ready to kill.

968
01:11:03.593 --> 01:11:06.720
So he called that death row.

969
01:11:06.720 --> 01:11:11.932
So I had these chickens out here during the hurricane.

970
01:11:11.932 --> 01:11:15.059
We killed a chicken that day.

971
01:11:15.059 --> 01:11:16.622
And cooked it.

972
01:11:16.622 --> 01:11:21.313
I got gas in my outdoor kitchen out there.

973
01:11:21.313 --> 01:11:26.524
See the big silver tank in the front of that?

974
01:11:26.524 --> 01:11:27.046
<v ARSENEAULT>Yes.

975
01:11:27.046 --> 01:11:28.609
<v LEE>Well, that’s butane.

976
01:11:28.609 --> 01:11:31.215
So this is all electric.

977
01:11:31.215 --> 01:11:36.948
I said when this go out, we can cook out there.

978
01:11:36.948 --> 01:11:40.075
And same thing during Hurricane Audrey.

979
01:11:40.075 --> 01:11:42.160
I was in Mossville.

980
01:11:42.160 --> 01:11:45.808
<v ARSENEAULT>What did you all do during that?

981
01:11:45.808 --> 01:11:58.837
<v LEE>We was at one of my aunt’s houses, and every day they killed two chickens because the crowd, the number of people that were there.

982
01:11:58.837 --> 01:12:08.739
And when auntie [unclear] her sister Cole and she was a cousin of mine but we called her aunt.

983
01:12:08.739 --> 01:12:14.472
And we killed two chickens a day and they cooked them.

984
01:12:14.472 --> 01:12:18.121
During Hurricane Audrey, no electric, no power.

985
01:12:18.121 --> 01:12:20.205
And same thing here.

986
01:12:20.205 --> 01:12:23.332
<v ARSENEAULT>Did Mossville flood during Hurricane Audrey?

987
01:12:23.332 --> 01:12:30.629
<v LEE>No. Some parts of it flooded, but not where anybody’s life was in danger.

988
01:12:30.629 --> 01:12:35.319
Some of the low-lying areas flooded, the roads flooded.

989
01:12:35.319 --> 01:12:47.827
But I think this last storm, whatever, Katrina and Rita and all of them, it was worse then than it was during Hurricane Audrey.

990
01:12:47.827 --> 01:13:00.857
What made Audrey so bad, and this was bad, too, but it took the service to get the lights and stuff back on, it took

991
01:13:00.857 --> 01:13:01.378
<c.p>longer.

992
01:13:01.378 --> 01:13:06.068
But everybody over there mostly had gas and electric.

993
01:13:06.068 --> 01:13:11.280
It wasn’t like here, this house here is totally electric.

994
01:13:11.280 --> 01:13:14.928
You’ve got your fireplaces for the wintertime.

995
01:13:14.928 --> 01:13:22.225
But for cooking and survival back then in Mossville, you had gas, natural gas.

996
01:13:22.225 --> 01:13:29.000
And Big Mama and them had a gas light in the yard.

997
01:13:29.000 --> 01:13:29.944
<v ARSENEAULT>Like kerosene?

998
01:13:29.944 --> 01:13:33.720
<v LEE>No, not, we had some kerosene lamps, too.

999
01:13:33.720 --> 01:13:44.104
But they had a gas lamp say like that bell, it was piped from, by the sidewalk there by the front door.

1000
01:13:44.104 --> 01:13:53.072
Like now we’ve got the big flood lights and stuff out there, where they had a gas light outside.

1001
01:13:53.072 --> 01:13:56.376
And we could see in the house.

1002
01:13:56.376 --> 01:14:00.152
It took, a lot of people had it.

1003
01:14:00.152 --> 01:14:11.480
And they had electricity, because I remember my grandfather saying if you ain’t studying or doing something constructive, there’s lights on in the house.

1004
01:14:11.480 --> 01:14:15.728
No, no. No, no. You better be reading something.

1005
01:14:15.728 --> 01:14:17.616
Getting your head right.

1006
01:14:17.616 --> 01:14:23.280
That TV, you’re catching the news, or some kinds of sports activity.

1007
01:14:23.280 --> 01:14:32.248
So for that acting and all of that, no. He said, all them lights come on for something constructive.

1008
01:14:32.248 --> 01:14:39.328
Now if you want to read a book, you could light up the whole house.

1009
01:14:39.328 --> 01:14:49.240
But if you just want to sit around there and watch TV, no, no, no, no. You turn them lights out.

1010
01:14:49.240 --> 01:14:54.904
Because they got enough light coming off that TV to see enough.

1011
01:14:54.904 --> 01:14:59.152
And you think about those things, it made sense.

1012
01:14:59.152 --> 01:15:09.064
And my grandpa used to tell me, he’d say, “Son, you see that TV, that light, that gas and all that?”

1013
01:15:09.064 --> 01:15:19.448
He say, “One day that’s going to come to where it’s going to be just as expensive as you pay house note.”

1014
01:15:19.448 --> 01:15:22.280
I said, “No, no way, man.”

1015
01:15:22.280 --> 01:15:25.112
He said, “I’m telling you, son.”

1016
01:15:25.112 --> 01:15:30.304
He said, “I’m not going to see it in my time.”

1017
01:15:30.304 --> 01:15:41.632
But he said, “You’re going to see it in your time, to where your gas bill, your light bill, and the gas for your

1018
01:15:41.632 --> 01:15:48.712
<c.p>automobile, is going to cost you just as much or more as your house note.”

1019
01:15:48.712 --> 01:15:51.544
He said, “Don’t think about insurance.

1020
01:15:51.544 --> 01:15:53.904
Don’t even think about it.”

1021
01:15:53.904 --> 01:15:56.264
I lived to see that.

1022
01:15:56.264 --> 01:16:00.512
He say, “You get through paying for your house.

1023
01:16:00.512 --> 01:16:06.176
But as long as you live, those other things, your insurance, lights,

1024
01:16:06.176 --> 01:16:13.256
or gas for your automobile or your house, you’ll never get through paying for it.”

1025
01:16:13.256 --> 01:16:20.336
He said, “You’re going to die owing one of them or all three of them.”

1026
01:16:20.336 --> 01:16:26.000
And you know but damn if that old man wasn’t right.

1027
01:16:26.000 --> 01:16:27.580
<v ARSENEAULT>It’s expensive now.

1028
01:16:27.580 --> 01:16:36.009
<v LEE>Just, you know, it’s the same with all kind of crap, you know what I mean?

1029
01:16:36.009 --> 01:16:39.170
I said, this old man crazy.

1030
01:16:39.170 --> 01:16:45.491
But he said, “They got one in every family, and you’re mine.”

1031
01:16:45.491 --> 01:16:49.179
He said, “You’re going to do good.

1032
01:16:49.179 --> 01:16:54.446
You might not be the brightest bulb in the class.

1033
01:16:54.446 --> 01:16:57.607
But you’re going to light up.”

1034
01:16:57.607 --> 01:17:00.768
And he said, “Can’t always get.

1035
01:17:00.768 --> 01:17:06.562
Get all you can while you can from who you can.”

1036
01:17:06.562 --> 01:17:13.411
And he said, “When you get old enough to retire, you’ll be set.”

1037
01:17:13.411 --> 01:17:16.045
He said the can thing.

1038
01:17:16.045 --> 01:17:25.000
If you can get out there and you can, and work and save some money, you can.

1039
01:17:25.000 --> 01:17:26.054
You know?

1040
01:17:26.054 --> 01:17:29.741
And he said it’s a can thing.

1041
01:17:29.741 --> 01:17:35.009
People going to come get you in their white coat.

1042
01:17:35.009 --> 01:17:39.750
They’re going to take you in, because you’re crazy.

1043
01:17:39.750 --> 01:17:49.232
But all the time, he was trying to prepare me for what was going on in the world.

1044
01:17:49.232 --> 01:17:52.393
And what it’s going to be.

1045
01:17:52.393 --> 01:17:58.188
And he told me some things that I’ve seen come true.

1046
01:17:58.188 --> 01:18:09.777
He said, “When I grew up, the [sun here?], in Bayou D’Inde and Mossville, it was Shoat’s Prairie, I think it was.

1047
01:18:09.777 --> 01:18:11.884
It was amazing to . . .

1048
01:18:11.884 --> 01:18:17.152
<v ARSENEAULT>What brought him to, what brought your family to Mossville?

1049
01:18:17.152 --> 01:18:20.839
Do you know when they first came?

1050
01:18:20.839 --> 01:18:24.000
<v LEE>Well, they got several stories.

1051
01:18:24.000 --> 01:18:24.563
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1052
01:18:24.563 --> 01:18:29.633
<v LEE>Now, my grandfather Lemelle came here through [a farmer?]

1053
01:18:29.633 --> 01:18:30.197
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1054
01:18:30.197 --> 01:18:30.760
<v LEE>Okay?

1055
01:18:30.760 --> 01:18:39.210
My grandfather, my dad’s people, they say, came here on the boat with [Jean] Lafitte.

1056
01:18:39.210 --> 01:18:40.337
<v ARSENEAULT>The pirate?!

1057
01:18:40.337 --> 01:18:42.027
<v LEE>That’s a saying.

1058
01:18:42.027 --> 01:18:42.590
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1059
01:18:42.590 --> 01:18:47.660
Just want to make sure it’s the same guy.

1060
01:18:47.660 --> 01:18:48.223
<v LEE>Yeah.

1061
01:18:48.223 --> 01:18:55.547
And they could not say Lee Lafitte, so they just called him Lee.

1062
01:18:55.547 --> 01:19:01.180
So he was Lafitte’s, my great-great-grandfather, was Lafitte’s cabin boy.

1063
01:19:01.180 --> 01:19:01.743
Slave.

1064
01:19:01.743 --> 01:19:07.940
And they got off the boat down there in Bayou D’Inde.

1065
01:19:07.940 --> 01:19:21.460
Because all my ancestors on my dad’s side was tied in with boats and water, worked on the boats, did that and did that.

1066
01:19:21.460 --> 01:19:24.840
And this is what they say.

1067
01:19:24.840 --> 01:19:29.347
They came here on the boat with Lafitte.

1068
01:19:29.347 --> 01:19:35.543
And they used to talk about digging this, and digging treasure.

1069
01:19:35.543 --> 01:19:40.613
<c.p>My grandfather had a lot of old gold pieces.

1070
01:19:40.613 --> 01:19:42.303
Lots of them.

1071
01:19:42.303 --> 01:19:47.373
And I got my hands on some of them.

1072
01:19:47.373 --> 01:19:51.880
And in the eighteen, 1700s, some gold pieces.

1073
01:19:51.880 --> 01:20:00.893
And he had a set of cufflinks out of five dollar gold pieces, my grandfather did.

1074
01:20:00.893 --> 01:20:02.583
I got them.

1075
01:20:02.583 --> 01:20:12.160
And I got some, like I don't know what country, you can’t hardly see where it’s from.

1076
01:20:12.160 --> 01:20:19.483
And got it, and I got some fifty-dollar gold pieces, some ten-dollar gold.

1077
01:20:19.483 --> 01:20:23.427
They’re worth thousands and thousands of dollars.

1078
01:20:23.427 --> 01:20:31.877
I had one of them checked, and a man offered me 2500 dollars for it.

1079
01:20:31.877 --> 01:20:35.257
It was a ten-dollar gold piece.

1080
01:20:35.257 --> 01:20:43.143
And I told him no. And I got them in a safe deposit box.

1081
01:20:43.143 --> 01:20:52.157
But I got the cuff links here, because I wanted to show it to my son.

1082
01:20:52.157 --> 01:20:54.410
He didn’t believe me.

1083
01:20:54.410 --> 01:20:57.227
I said, “I’ll show you.”

1084
01:20:57.227 --> 01:21:05.677
I said, “Boy, your grandfather, his dad wore these.” [unclear] And that’s what they say,

1085
01:21:05.677 --> 01:21:13.000
and that’s why Grandpa and them, all of them stayed around the . . .

1086
01:21:13.000 --> 01:21:14.112
<v ARSENEAULT>The water.

1087
01:21:14.112 --> 01:21:15.224
<v LEE>The water.

1088
01:21:15.224 --> 01:21:22.454
And my great grandmother and them ran what they called the Westlake Laundry.

1089
01:21:22.454 --> 01:21:24.678
She was a Cotloeng.

1090
01:21:24.678 --> 01:21:26.346
Jeanne Baptiste Cotloeng.

1091
01:21:26.346 --> 01:21:36.912
I’ve got a picture of them, her and her three sisters, washing clothes out in the bayou down there.

1092
01:21:36.912 --> 01:21:40.249
They call it the Westlake Laundry.

1093
01:21:40.249 --> 01:21:49.146
And they would wash clothes for these logging camps, when they was logging back through here.

1094
01:21:49.146 --> 01:21:55.820
And Mom, Aunt [Pinky?] and Aunt Minerva, there were some old midwives.

1095
01:21:55.820 --> 01:22:02.493
They’d go live with a woman that’s going to have a baby.

1096
01:22:02.493 --> 01:22:08.054
They’d go there and help the woman deliver the baby.

1097
01:22:08.054 --> 01:22:20.844
There’s a lot of boys and young men I knew that were older than me, white and black, they knew who I was,

1098
01:22:20.844 --> 01:22:31.966
I was a descendant of Mom, everybody called her Mom, because she brought a lot of them in the world.

1099
01:22:31.966 --> 01:22:33.078
You know?

1100
01:22:33.078 --> 01:22:36.971
And that’s, that was my dad’s grandmother.

1101
01:22:36.971 --> 01:22:47.537
<c.p>So they was supposed to have been like her parents were from Ethiopia, where they came in there from.

1102
01:22:47.537 --> 01:22:51.429
I’m the runt compared to my brother.

1103
01:22:51.429 --> 01:22:55.878
And they were all some big, tall people.

1104
01:22:55.878 --> 01:22:58.659
My grandfather was about six-three.

1105
01:22:58.659 --> 01:23:01.995
My dad was about six-four, six-five.

1106
01:23:01.995 --> 01:23:07.000
I had some cousins seven foot, six-eight, six-nine.

1107
01:23:07.000 --> 01:23:07.600
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

1108
01:23:07.600 --> 01:23:09.400
<v LEE>So it’s interesting.

1109
01:23:09.400 --> 01:23:17.800
And the other story is like they came in here after the Civil War.

1110
01:23:17.800 --> 01:23:23.800
And they came off of Robert E. Lee’s farm [plantation].

1111
01:23:23.800 --> 01:23:26.800
And they came through here.

1112
01:23:26.800 --> 01:23:34.000
And they settled back there on the bayou, met that Cotloeng woman.

1113
01:23:34.000 --> 01:23:41.200
And Jeanne Baptiste Cotloeng had 1600 acres back there along the bayou.

1114
01:23:41.200 --> 01:23:47.200
And he gave each one of his kids 200 acres.

1115
01:23:47.200 --> 01:23:53.800
And Mom happened to be our attachment to the 1600 acres.

1116
01:23:53.800 --> 01:23:56.800
And she became a Lee.

1117
01:23:56.800 --> 01:24:01.600
And my grandfather’s daddy was Jim Lee, too.

1118
01:24:01.600 --> 01:24:05.200
My grandfather was Jim Lee, Junior.

1119
01:24:05.200 --> 01:24:10.600
So that, I don't know, I hear both stories.

1120
01:24:10.600 --> 01:24:16.000
My grandfather used to tell me both of them.

1121
01:24:16.000 --> 01:24:20.200
And he said, “Now, I don't know.

1122
01:24:20.200 --> 01:24:24.400
One of the two is the truth.

1123
01:24:24.400 --> 01:24:35.800
And one is,” he’d say, but he said, “I’ve got the old money, that he came on the boat,”

1124
01:24:35.800 --> 01:24:46.000
it’s been, the thing is that he came in here with Mr. Porter and all of them.

1125
01:24:46.000 --> 01:24:54.400
And they, when [Rigmaiden?], grandfather that I knew, he looked like a white man.

1126
01:24:54.400 --> 01:25:00.400
They say he was a [heir?] with Robert E. Lee.

1127
01:25:00.400 --> 01:25:05.800
Now I don't know which story is the truth.

1128
01:25:05.800 --> 01:25:10.000
But my dad’s mama is real light.

1129
01:25:10.000 --> 01:25:13.000
You seen the picture?

1130
01:25:13.000 --> 01:25:15.579
<v ARSENEAULT>On the fireplace over there?

1131
01:25:15.579 --> 01:25:19.189
He’s always trying to remember that quote.

1132
01:25:19.189 --> 01:25:19.705
<v LEE>Yeah.

1133
01:25:19.705 --> 01:25:23.831
Well, anyway, she’s real bright, light and bright.

1134
01:25:23.831 --> 01:25:27.441
Damn near white, I used to tell.

1135
01:25:27.441 --> 01:25:28.988
It’s a mixture.

1136
01:25:28.988 --> 01:25:31.051
It’s a Creole mixture.

1137
01:25:31.051 --> 01:25:34.661
<v ARSENEAULT>Are there other Creole families in Mossville?

1138
01:25:34.661 --> 01:25:35.693
<v LEE>Oh, yeah!

1139
01:25:35.693 --> 01:25:41.366
Everybody in Mossville is mix, that I know of, you know.

1140
01:25:41.366 --> 01:25:46.524
And they don’t, a lot of them won’t claim it.

1141
01:25:46.524 --> 01:25:53.744
But you know, a lot of them, most of them come from down east.

1142
01:25:53.744 --> 01:25:55.291
<v ARSENEAULT>Where’s down east?

1143
01:25:55.291 --> 01:25:55.807
<v LEE>Mamou.

1144
01:25:55.807 --> 01:25:56.839
New Iberia.

1145
01:25:56.839 --> 01:26:00.965
See, Papa Lemelle and them came from Leonville.

1146
01:26:00.965 --> 01:26:01.480
Okay?

1147
01:26:01.480 --> 01:26:01.996
Leonville.

1148
01:26:01.996 --> 01:26:05.091
It’s over there off the bayou.

1149
01:26:05.091 --> 01:26:13.858
And then they moved to New Iberia, his daddy did, to work in the mines and stuff.

1150
01:26:13.858 --> 01:26:20.563
And Papa Lemelle, went to work, his daddy died, he went to work.

1151
01:26:20.563 --> 01:26:24.173
He was working in the oil field.

1152
01:26:24.173 --> 01:26:24.689
Cooking.

1153
01:26:24.689 --> 01:26:26.236
He was young.

1154
01:26:26.236 --> 01:26:38.098
And the man who owned the oil rig tasted some of the food that he was cooking and said, “Hey, who did this?

1155
01:26:38.098 --> 01:26:39.646
Who cooked this?”

1156
01:26:39.646 --> 01:26:42.740
They say, “Well, Edward cooked this.”

1157
01:26:42.740 --> 01:26:49.961
So he asked Papa would he come to work with him in Lake Charles,

1158
01:26:49.961 --> 01:26:59.244
and he would see that he had a place to stay and everything, and establish him over there.

1159
01:26:59.244 --> 01:27:02.854
He said, well, and so he came.

1160
01:27:02.854 --> 01:27:09.559
So that’s kind of a story that how the Lemelles got to Mossville.

1161
01:27:09.559 --> 01:27:13.685
And that was like with the Bell estate.

1162
01:27:13.685 --> 01:27:18.843
Old man Bell, [unclear] oil and gas and all, multimillionaire.

1163
01:27:18.843 --> 01:27:24.000
So he worked for a family, sixty-six some years.

1164
01:27:24.000 --> 01:27:24.430
<v ARSENEAULT>Cooking?

1165
01:27:24.430 --> 01:27:25.721
<v LEE>Cooking and overseeing.

1166
01:27:25.721 --> 01:27:29.592
Because they had a house here in Lake Charles.

1167
01:27:29.592 --> 01:27:32.604
They had a house in Big Lake.

1168
01:27:32.604 --> 01:27:35.185
They had a farm in Hacker.

1169
01:27:35.185 --> 01:27:45.079
I’m talking, you know, talking money like David Junior was at LSU and his aunty died, and she left him twenty-five million dollars.

1170
01:27:45.079 --> 01:27:46.800
That kind of money.

1171
01:27:46.800 --> 01:27:48.951
You know what I’m saying?

1172
01:27:48.951 --> 01:27:49.381
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

1173
01:27:49.381 --> 01:27:51.532
<v LEE>Yeah, that kind of money.

1174
01:27:51.532 --> 01:27:54.543
The aunty died, leave me twenty-five million.

1175
01:27:54.543 --> 01:27:54.974
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

1176
01:27:54.974 --> 01:27:58.415
I need that to happen to me. (laughs)

1177
01:27:58.415 --> 01:28:00.136
<v LEE>That must be nice.

1178
01:28:00.136 --> 01:28:10.030
But anyway, Papa, he stayed in Lake Charles a little while and then he bought that house, got that house there in Mossville.

1179
01:28:10.030 --> 01:28:12.181
And some of them Cotloengs . . .

1180
01:28:12.181 --> 01:28:14.332
<v ARSENEAULT>How do you spell that?

1181
01:28:14.332 --> 01:28:16.483
What is that last word?

1182
01:28:16.483 --> 01:28:17.343
Is that . . .

1183
01:28:17.343 --> 01:28:17.774
<v LEE>Cotloeng?

1184
01:28:17.774 --> 01:28:18.204
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

1185
01:28:18.204 --> 01:28:20.355
Is it a last name?

1186
01:28:20.355 --> 01:28:20.785
<v LEE>C-o-t-l-o-e-n-g.

1187
01:28:20.785 --> 01:28:23.796
And some of them spell it C-a-t

1188
01:28:23.796 --> 01:28:24.226
<v ARSENEAULT>C-a-t?

1189
01:28:24.226 --> 01:28:24.657
<v LEE>Yeah.

1190
01:28:24.657 --> 01:28:25.087
L-o-n-g.

1191
01:28:25.087 --> 01:28:25.517
C-o-t

1192
01:28:25.517 --> 01:28:25.947
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1193
01:28:25.947 --> 01:28:26.377
<v LEE>Yeah.

1194
01:28:26.377 --> 01:28:34.551
And some of them, back when grandfather, he’d tell you, it all depend on, this is off the record,

1195
01:28:34.551 --> 01:28:41.004
he said it all depended on what white man that slavery that you belong to.

1196
01:28:41.004 --> 01:28:47.026
Some of them spelled it like this, some of them spelled it like that.

1197
01:28:47.026 --> 01:28:53.049
And a lot of them were telling them what their name was, he said,

1198
01:28:53.049 --> 01:28:58.642
and a lot of people had different accents back in the old days.

1199
01:28:58.642 --> 01:29:05.094
And you might tell them one way, but you didn’t know how to spell it.

1200
01:29:05.094 --> 01:29:07.675
So they wrote it like that.

1201
01:29:07.675 --> 01:29:10.257
And then you went with that.

1202
01:29:10.257 --> 01:29:13.698
Because they say like the Towners and Tanners.

1203
01:29:13.698 --> 01:29:18.000
Yeah, my grandfather say it all depend on what . . .

1204
01:29:18.000 --> 01:29:19.749
<v ARSENEAULT>It’s the same family?

1205
01:29:19.749 --> 01:29:21.061
It all depends?

1206
01:29:21.061 --> 01:29:21.498
<v LEE>Yeah.

1207
01:29:21.498 --> 01:29:26.308
Some people say it is, and some people say it ain’t.

1208
01:29:26.308 --> 01:29:29.806
But some of the Tanners was our uncles.

1209
01:29:29.806 --> 01:29:32.430
So I don't know, you know?

1210
01:29:32.430 --> 01:29:35.053
I just, I called them cousin.

1211
01:29:35.053 --> 01:29:38.114
You know, I’d say we all related.

1212
01:29:38.114 --> 01:29:41.175
Because what my grandfather said, makes sense.

1213
01:29:41.175 --> 01:29:48.608
And he said some of them married their cousins, who had nobody else you had to marry.

1214
01:29:48.608 --> 01:29:52.981
And he said they don’t come to the family reunion.

1215
01:29:52.981 --> 01:29:56.479
They say they’re not Towners because they’re Tanners.

1216
01:29:56.479 --> 01:30:00.852
You see, because they’re ashamed because they married their cousin.

1217
01:30:00.852 --> 01:30:01.726
You know?

1218
01:30:01.726 --> 01:30:04.350
Hey, I don't know, you know?

1219
01:30:04.350 --> 01:30:06.536
It makes sense, you know?

1220
01:30:06.536 --> 01:30:12.221
How can this man over here be my uncle and he’s a Tanner?

1221
01:30:12.221 --> 01:30:16.156
And my grandmother’s a Towner, but that’s uncle’s kid.

1222
01:30:16.156 --> 01:30:19.217
You know, that’s Uncle so and so.

1223
01:30:19.217 --> 01:30:27.087
Well it’s not, it wasn’t just a thing like, you know, everybody was Uncle this and Uncle that.

1224
01:30:27.087 --> 01:30:28.837
But this over here

1225
01:30:28.837 --> 01:30:32.772
<c.p>went a little deeper, you know what I’m saying?

1226
01:30:32.772 --> 01:30:37.144
You can’t talk to her, because that’s Uncle Bob’s daughter.

1227
01:30:37.144 --> 01:30:38.894
And she’s a Prater.

1228
01:30:38.894 --> 01:30:41.517
And you’re kin to the Praters.

1229
01:30:41.517 --> 01:30:48.076
And no, they say I’m not kin to the Praters, I’m kin to the Tanners.

1230
01:30:48.076 --> 01:30:58.570
Yeah, yeah, okay, okay, go marry her and have a kid and the head will be on backwards, and then you’ll know, you know?

1231
01:30:58.570 --> 01:31:01.194
All kind of that stuff, man.

1232
01:31:01.194 --> 01:31:03.817
But that was Mossville, you know?

1233
01:31:03.817 --> 01:31:08.190
And it was . . . I know you got enough of it.

1234
01:31:08.190 --> 01:31:13.000
You have any other question you want to ask me?

1235
01:31:13.000 --> 01:31:16.442
<v ARSENEAULT>I have a lot of, we’re at an hour and a half.

1236
01:31:16.442 --> 01:31:17.589
So we can stop.

1237
01:31:17.589 --> 01:31:21.605
But is it okay if we come back and ask a few more questions?

1238
01:31:21.605 --> 01:31:26.481
I have, I want to talk about the club, I want to talk about the Paradise Club.

1239
01:31:26.481 --> 01:31:27.341
<v LEE>About the Paradise?

1240
01:31:27.341 --> 01:31:28.202
<v ARSENEAULT>And the pub.

1241
01:31:28.202 --> 01:31:29.636
The one that you ran.

1242
01:31:29.636 --> 01:31:30.783
You ran the pub.

1243
01:31:30.783 --> 01:31:31.930
We’ll talk about that.

1244
01:31:31.930 --> 01:31:32.217
<v LEE>Yeah.

1245
01:31:32.217 --> 01:31:32.504
Yeah.

1246
01:31:32.504 --> 01:31:33.651
<v ARSENEAULT>We’ll talk about that.

1247
01:31:33.651 --> 01:31:36.806
<v LEE>Yeah, they had the pub, and they had the Cowboy Inn.

1248
01:31:36.806 --> 01:31:39.961
Peter Deville, and Rodney Caesar, and then they had Caesar’s Inn.

1249
01:31:39.961 --> 01:31:42.256
<v ARSENEAULT>So I didn’t know about any of those.

1250
01:31:42.256 --> 01:31:42.543
Okay.

1251
01:31:42.543 --> 01:31:44.837
We can come back and talk about that.

1252
01:31:44.837 --> 01:31:47.419
And what else did I want to talk about?

1253
01:31:47.419 --> 01:31:50.000
<v LEE>Man, the Paradise had big name entertainment, man.

1254
01:31:50.000 --> 01:31:57.091
<v ARSENEAULT>That’s what I wanted to, the music in Mossville, I want to talk about that.

1255
01:31:57.091 --> 01:32:01.345
<v LEE>The old, what they call like the Chitlin Circuit.

1256
01:32:01.345 --> 01:32:02.291
<v ARSENEAULT>What’s that?

1257
01:32:02.291 --> 01:32:05.600
<v LEE>The black groups, the famous black groups.

1258
01:32:05.600 --> 01:32:08.909
And they called it the Chitlin Circuit.

1259
01:32:08.909 --> 01:32:10.327
<v ARSENEAULT>After the food?

1260
01:32:10.327 --> 01:32:11.745
<v LEE>After the food.

1261
01:32:11.745 --> 01:32:12.218
Yeah.

1262
01:32:12.218 --> 01:32:12.691
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1263
01:32:12.691 --> 01:32:16.000
<v LEE>And Bobby Blue Bland, BB King.

1264
01:32:16.000 --> 01:32:16.373
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

1265
01:32:16.373 --> 01:32:17.118
<v LEE>Ray Charles.

1266
01:32:17.118 --> 01:32:19.353
<v ARSENEAULT>They played at the Paradise Club?

1267
01:32:19.353 --> 01:32:20.471
<v LEE>Yeah, they played.

1268
01:32:20.471 --> 01:32:21.216
<c.p>?: Etta James.

1269
01:32:21.216 --> 01:32:21.961
<v LEE>Etta James.

1270
01:32:21.961 --> 01:32:22.333
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

1271
01:32:22.333 --> 01:32:30.157
<v LEE>And you see, we was right next door, about two houses down, from the Paradise, where my brother lived, where Richard.

1272
01:32:30.157 --> 01:32:33.510
<v ARSENEAULT>So where did you all, where was your house?

1273
01:32:33.510 --> 01:32:35.000
<v LEE>On Prater Road.

1274
01:32:35.000 --> 01:32:35.473
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1275
01:32:35.473 --> 01:32:40.674
<v LEE>My house, myself, was across the street from the Mossville School.

1276
01:32:40.674 --> 01:32:41.146
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1277
01:32:41.146 --> 01:32:45.874
<v LEE>Now my mother and my brother stayed on Prater Road.

1278
01:32:45.874 --> 01:32:53.439
And then one time I stayed on Prater Road in one of my old uncle houses.

1279
01:32:53.439 --> 01:32:56.276
I got it after he passed.

1280
01:32:56.276 --> 01:32:58.167
<v ARSENEAULT>We know Prater Road.

1281
01:32:58.167 --> 01:33:00.531
We interviewed Mr. Haki [Vincent].

1282
01:33:00.531 --> 01:33:01.477
<v LEE>Mr. who?

1283
01:33:01.477 --> 01:33:02.423
<v ARSENEAULT>Mr. Haki?

1284
01:33:02.423 --> 01:33:02.895
Vincent?

1285
01:33:02.895 --> 01:33:05.259
Is that on Prater Road?

1286
01:33:05.259 --> 01:33:05.732
<v LEE>No.

1287
01:33:05.732 --> 01:33:08.096
<v ARSENEAULT>That’s not on Prater Road?

1288
01:33:08.096 --> 01:33:08.569
Oh.

1289
01:33:08.569 --> 01:33:09.515
Coach Williams.

1290
01:33:09.515 --> 01:33:10.460
Oh, okay.

1291
01:33:10.460 --> 01:33:14.243
<v LEE>It’s on Williams, down there around Coach Williams.

1292
01:33:14.243 --> 01:33:16.607
Did you all interview Coach?

1293
01:33:16.607 --> 01:33:17.552
<v ARSENEAULT>Not yet.

1294
01:33:17.552 --> 01:33:21.335
We interviewed him at the Oral History Day.

1295
01:33:21.335 --> 01:33:26.536
But we still have to go back for his . . .he’s busy.

1296
01:33:26.536 --> 01:33:28.900
He still works, I think.

1297
01:33:28.900 --> 01:33:29.372
<v LEE>Yeah.

1298
01:33:29.372 --> 01:33:33.155
He still work at the sheriff office, too.

1299
01:33:33.155 --> 01:33:33.628
<v ARSENEAULT>Yeah.

1300
01:33:33.628 --> 01:33:35.519
<v LEE>He’s on the board.

1301
01:33:35.519 --> 01:33:36.464
<v ARSENEAULT>He’s busy.

1302
01:33:36.464 --> 01:33:42.138
<v LEE>He was very instrumental in a lot of our lives, you know?

1303
01:33:42.138 --> 01:33:50.176
And he, you know, he was there with us, he started at Mossville at a young age.

1304
01:33:50.176 --> 01:33:55.377
And he had the experience out of the military, and college.

1305
01:33:55.377 --> 01:34:04.833
And I think Coach, if I’m not mistaken, he was one of the first black to graduate from McNeese University.

1306
01:34:04.833 --> 01:34:11.452
And he, you know, I looked up to him as a kid coming up.

1307
01:34:11.452 --> 01:34:16.653
Because I was fairly young when he started coaching at Mossville.

1308
01:34:16.653 --> 01:34:24.218
And he, yeah, he made a lot of history there with the football, basketball and track.

1309
01:34:24.218 --> 01:34:28.000
And tennis and the board of education.

1310
01:34:28.000 --> 01:34:32.755
<v ARSENEAULT>Where did he coach after they had to integrate?

1311
01:34:32.755 --> 01:34:35.397
Did they move him somewhere?

1312
01:34:35.397 --> 01:34:37.511
<v LEE>He went to Westlake.

1313
01:34:37.511 --> 01:34:38.039
<v ARSENEAULT>Okay.

1314
01:34:38.039 --> 01:34:38.568
<v LEE>Yeah.

1315
01:34:38.568 --> 01:34:42.795
But he was an assistant coach at Westlake.

1316
01:34:42.795 --> 01:34:44.380
I don't know.

1317
01:34:44.380 --> 01:34:45.437
You know.

1318
01:34:45.437 --> 01:34:48.607
Some people say that was wrong.

1319
01:34:48.607 --> 01:34:51.249
He had a better record.

1320
01:34:51.249 --> 01:34:51.777
<v ARSENEAULT>Politics?

1321
01:34:51.777 --> 01:34:53.362
<v LEE>Politics and everything.

1322
01:34:53.362 --> 01:35:03.402
But Coach hung in there with them, and he’s still hanging with them to prove that he is somebody.

1323
01:35:03.402 --> 01:35:06.572
And he, to be reckoned with.

1324
01:35:06.572 --> 01:35:09.214
Because he’s well-respected, you know.

1325
01:35:09.214 --> 01:35:12.913
And he mold a bunch of people.

1326
01:35:12.913 --> 01:35:16.083
Do you understand what I mean?

1327
01:35:16.083 --> 01:35:23.480
He said if I’ve got to mold you from your behind to your head,

1328
01:35:23.480 --> 01:35:30.349
I’m going to do what need to be done to make you successful.

1329
01:35:30.349 --> 01:35:34.576
And to make my program a winning program.

1330
01:35:34.576 --> 01:35:41.974
Because he was over the recreation department before he became the coach at school.

1331
01:35:41.974 --> 01:35:46.201
And he was from right there in Mossville.

1332
01:35:46.201 --> 01:35:55.183
In fact, where I built my first house, I purchased the land from Coach Williams, you know?

1333
01:35:55.183 --> 01:35:58.882
And that, his family owned that land.

1334
01:35:58.882 --> 01:36:02.052
I purchased some land from Coach.

1335
01:36:02.052 --> 01:36:10.507
In fact, his wife is related to me on my grandmother’s side, on the Lee side.

1336
01:36:10.507 --> 01:36:13.677
And he’s all right with me.

1337
01:36:13.677 --> 01:36:15.790
He’s a great fellow.

1338
01:36:15.790 --> 01:36:21.603
He deserved more than that road down there named after him.

1339
01:36:21.603 --> 01:36:29.000
Because the lives he didn’t touch through that school and the recreation is . . .

1340
01:36:29.000 --> 01:36:33.527
<v ARSENEAULT>We hear a lot of good things about Coach Williams.

1341
01:36:33.527 --> 01:36:33.980
<v LEE>Yeah.

1342
01:36:33.980 --> 01:36:36.243
He’s contributed to the community.

1343
01:36:36.243 --> 01:36:40.770
And I was the resident deputy there for 15 years.

1344
01:36:40.770 --> 01:36:41.676
In Mossville.

1345
01:36:41.676 --> 01:36:45.750
But I worked twenty-one years for the sheriff’s department.

1346
01:36:45.750 --> 01:36:48.014
<v ARSENEAULT>You worked the same time?

1347
01:36:48.014 --> 01:36:50.730
<v LEE>I worked two jobs, twenty-one years.

1348
01:36:50.730 --> 01:36:51.182
<v ARSENEAULT>Wow.

1349
01:36:51.182 --> 01:36:52.993
<v LEE>The same two jobs.

1350
01:36:52.993 --> 01:36:55.257
The sheriff’s office and Citgo.

1351
01:36:55.257 --> 01:36:58.878
And sixteen hours a day for twenty-one years.

1352
01:36:58.878 --> 01:37:02.500
And came back from Vietnam, I couldn’t sleep.

1353
01:37:02.500 --> 01:37:09.743
I went to work for the federal government and I went back to school at night.

1354
01:37:09.743 --> 01:37:11.554
I went to McNeese.

1355
01:37:11.554 --> 01:37:18.797
Things happened, and then I went to work for the sheriff’s office and the post office.

1356
01:37:18.797 --> 01:37:22.872
And they had that non-union trouble out at Citgo.

1357
01:37:22.872 --> 01:37:28.757
So the sheriff sent some of us out there to work at Citgo.

1358
01:37:28.757 --> 01:37:36.000
And while I was out there at Citgo, they offered me a job in security.

1359
01:37:36.000 --> 01:37:41.272
I said, “I don’t want to be no damn security guard.”

1360
01:37:41.272 --> 01:37:45.585
And they told me, “You got all the qualifications.

1361
01:37:45.585 --> 01:37:48.939
He say, “This ain’t no [regular] security.”

1362
01:37:48.939 --> 01:37:56.607
He said, “We’re paying,” I was working for the post office, I was making six dollars.

1363
01:37:56.607 --> 01:38:02.358
And he said, “You making seven dollar and fifty cent an hour.”

1364
01:38:02.358 --> 01:38:05.233
I said, “As a security guard?”

1365
01:38:05.233 --> 01:38:07.629
I said, “You kidding me?”

1366
01:38:07.629 --> 01:38:09.546
He said, “No, man.”

1367
01:38:09.546 --> 01:38:17.214
He said, “We’re supposed to have a negotiation on a contract and it should go up.”

1368
01:38:17.214 --> 01:38:22.006
He say, “You make fifteen cents less than an operator.”

1369
01:38:22.006 --> 01:38:31.112
So you had to have a college degree, two years of college and two years of law enforcement experience.

1370
01:38:31.112 --> 01:38:33.508
And he said, “You’re qualified.”

1371
01:38:33.508 --> 01:38:34.946
I said, “Yeah.”

1372
01:38:34.946 --> 01:38:38.780
He said, “Man, we can get you on.”

1373
01:38:38.780 --> 01:38:42.134
He said, “You got two weeks’ vacation.

1374
01:38:42.134 --> 01:38:47.885
You got your [savings and thrift, a 401k] plan, you got retirement.”

1375
01:38:47.885 --> 01:38:51.719
I said, “Man, where you can sign up?”

1376
01:38:51.719 --> 01:38:54.594
So I went to work there.

1377
01:38:54.594 --> 01:38:57.949
And I stayed out there twenty-one years.

1378
01:38:57.949 --> 01:39:00.824
And I retired, when was it?

1379
01:39:00.824 --> 01:39:01.304
Ninety-six?

1380
01:39:01.304 --> 01:39:02.741
<c.p>?: Two or three?

1381
01:39:02.741 --> 01:39:03.220
Ninety-three?

1382
01:39:03.220 --> 01:39:05.137
No, ’96, I guess.

1383
01:39:05.137 --> 01:39:06.096
<v LEE>Ninety-six, yeah.

1384
01:39:06.096 --> 01:39:08.492
I got hurt out there.

1385
01:39:08.492 --> 01:39:11.847
And I was already injured from Vietnam.

1386
01:39:11.847 --> 01:39:17.118
And I couldn’t pass my physical to go back to work.

1387
01:39:17.118 --> 01:39:20.473
So it kind of messed with me.

1388
01:39:20.473 --> 01:39:22.390
But they retired me

1389
01:39:22.390 --> 01:39:24.786
<c.p>just like I was sixty-five.

1390
01:39:24.786 --> 01:39:31.016
And the VA changed my disability and I went to 100 percent disabled.

1391
01:39:31.016 --> 01:39:37.725
But just like when I told that lady, she said, “Where you work at?”

1392
01:39:37.725 --> 01:39:42.038
And I was filling out for my Social Security.

1393
01:39:42.038 --> 01:39:46.351
I said, “I worked ten years for the government.”

1394
01:39:46.351 --> 01:39:51.623
I said, “I worked ten years part-time in the club thing.”

1395
01:39:51.623 --> 01:40:01.208
And I said, “I got twenty-one years at the sheriff’s office,” and I say, “I got twenty-one years at Citgo.”

1396
01:40:01.208 --> 01:40:04.083
She said, “How old are you?”

1397
01:40:04.083 --> 01:40:06.000
I told her . . .

1398
01:40:06.000 --> 01:40:07.450
<v ARSENEAULT>It didn’t add up.

1399
01:40:07.450 --> 01:40:07.813
<v LEE>Yeah.

1400
01:40:07.813 --> 01:40:09.626
Something ain’t right about this.

1401
01:40:09.626 --> 01:40:13.251
I said, “No, I worked those two jobs twenty-one years.”

1402
01:40:13.251 --> 01:40:15.789
I said, “I always had two jobs.”

1403
01:40:15.789 --> 01:40:17.602
And she shook her head.

1404
01:40:17.602 --> 01:40:20.503
She said, something was wrong with the math.

1405
01:40:20.503 --> 01:40:21.953
And I say, yeah.

1406
01:40:21.953 --> 01:40:30.655
I said, “I was just waiting on your jobs, looking at you, and you’re still writing that down, and you looked up at me.”

1407
01:40:30.655 --> 01:40:32.468
So but I did it.

1408
01:40:32.468 --> 01:40:34.643
<v ARSENEAULT>Well, we’re almost at two hours.

1409
01:40:34.643 --> 01:40:38.994
I would hate to take up any more of you all’s time.

1410
01:40:38.994 --> 01:40:42.982
But if you’re interested, we can talk some more next time.

1411
01:40:42.982 --> 01:40:46.246
<v LEE>Yeah, I like to talk about the Paradise Club.

1412
01:40:46.246 --> 01:40:48.784
<v ARSENEAULT>I would love to talk about that.

1413
01:40:48.784 --> 01:40:51.322
<v LEE>I waited tables there as a youngster.

1414
01:40:51.322 --> 01:40:52.047
<v ARSENEAULT>Oh, perfect!

1415
01:40:52.047 --> 01:40:58.211
Okay, I’m going to put that in the notes, because we’re going to ask you about that.

1416
01:40:58.211 --> 01:40:58.573
<v LEE>Yeah.

1417
01:40:58.573 --> 01:40:58.936
Yeah.

1418
01:40:58.936 --> 01:41:02.561
<v ARSENEAULT>And thank you so much for sharing this with us.

1419
01:41:02.561 --> 01:41:04.374
We really do appreciate it.

1420
01:41:04.374 --> 01:41:08.000
And we’ll set something up with you next time.

1421
01:41:08.000 --> 01:41:08.333
<c.p>[End 4531.

1422
01:41:08.333 --> 01:41:08.833
End Session I.]
